{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'cat /Users/lchingen/hivemind/clean-arch/e2e-comparison-2026-11-13/full-trace/trace-reader-brief.md /Users/lchingen/hivemind/clean-arch/e2e-comparison-2026-11-13/full-trace/trace-packet-r1.json'","aggregated_output":"# The loss trace: was the fact extracted? (read-only)\n\nYou are an independent reader. Do not edit files, run model calls or start runs, and do not read any file other than this brief and the one\npacket you are given.\n\nEach point of your packet holds one **decisive item**: a statement an earlier reader, called reader B, cited for a frozen answer (its day,\ntitle, who said it, the passage and its meaning). Beside it is **every evidence item a system kept from that same article** (each with a\n`ref`, a summary and the passages it quotes); the list is empty when the system kept nothing from the article.\n\nFor each point, answer one question: does one of the kept items **state the decisive item's fact** — the same fact or figure, in any wording?\nName the kept item's `ref` that states it (the first if several do), or `none` when no kept item states it. Search the kept items for the\nfigure or the fact; do not name an item because it is related, from the same article, or about the same topic.\n\nReturn one JSON object per point, one per line, in packet order, and nothing else (no prose, no code fence):\n`{\"point\": 3, \"extracted\": \"<ref>\"|\"none\", \"why\": \"one sentence naming the figure or fact that decides it\"}`.\n[\n {\n  \"point\": 1,\n  \"decisive_item\": {\n   \"day\": \"2026-07-06\",\n   \"title\": \"Nvidia GPU Debt Backstop Unleashes the AI Project Trinity: Capital, Offtake and Datacenters\",\n   \"who\": \"Sharon AI (counterparty disclosure, reported by SemiAnalysis)\",\n   \"passage\": \"Sharon disclosed a total backstop value of $4.88B\",\n   \"meaning\": {\n    \"who\": \"Sharon AI disclosed\",\n    \"what\": \"total value of NVIDIA's backstop on Sharon's 72MW Australian AI factory (up to 40,000 GB300)\",\n    \"how_much\": \"$4.88B; implied floor ~$2.33/hr/GPU\",\n    \"period\": \"six years\",\n    \"condition\": \"up to 40,000 GB300s\",\n    \"status\": \"happened\",\n    \"status_note\": \"announced June 2026\"\n   }\n  },\n  \"kept_from_that_article\": [\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e1\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis said that, three years into the AI buildout, the general market's Nvidia shipment-volume and revenue estimates for the second half of 2026 remain materially below its own supply-chain tracking in its Accelerator Model, even as Nvidia revenue is rising sharply.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Nvidia revenue is skyrocketing, and even 3 years into the build out, the general market is still materially lower on shipment volumes and revenue estimates for Nvidia in the 2nd half of this year versus our through supply chain tracking in the Accelerator Model .\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e2\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis forecast that AI debt financing will grow to more than $7 trillion outstanding by 2029, making it the second-largest asset-backed debt market after the roughly $13 trillion US mortgage-backed market, and that annual AI capex will exceed $2 trillion in 2028 with cumulative 2024–2029 AI capex of about $11.1 trillion, mainly funded by credit markets.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"AI Debt Financing will become a multi-trillion-dollar credit market, with over $7T of debt outstanding by 2029 driven both by AI IT Capex and AI Datacenter Capex needs for Neoclouds, datacenter builders, and even hyperscalers. This will make it the second largest asset backed debt market after the US mortgage-backed financing market at just over $13T.\\nAnnual AI Capex – including GPUs, networking, storage and attached CPU compute as well as for the Datacenters to house AI compute – will be well north of $2T in 2028. Cumulative AI Capex from 2024 to 2029 will reach ~$11.1T, and credit markets will be the main funding source for this buildout.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e3\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis reported that Nvidia has begun backstopping GPU rental offtakes, providing Neoclouds a take-or-pay commitment and minimum revenue guarantee on underlying GPU capacity while taking a share of Neocloud revenue earned above the backstop level.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"This is why Nvidia has stepped in and has started backstopping GPU rental offtakes themselves . In the backstop program, Nvidia provides a take-or-pay commitment to Neoclouds – a minimum revenue guarantee on the underlying GPU capacity. In exchange for this backstop, Nvidia also shares in a portion of the Neocloud’s revenue earned above the backstop level.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e4\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis said Nvidia has gone beyond GPU offtake backstops and has started backstopping datacenter leases as well, to help Neoclouds secure datacenter capacity.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Datacenter: However, even with the backstop in place, securing the final leg of the Trinity and obtaining datacenter capacity remains challenging absent a creative datacenter rental structure or resorting to a self-built datacenter. Here, Nvidia is diving even deeper, as it has started backstopping datacenter leases.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e5\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis described Nvidia's backstop program as typically six years, with Nvidia standing ready to buy compute at pre-agreed price levels that vary over time and each Neocloud negotiating individual revenue-share and backstop schedules; it presented an illustrative lower-end backstop pricing curve averaging $2.36 per GPU-hour over six years and said it expects most Neoclouds to negotiate higher backstops.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Nvidia’s backstop program is typically six years in length, and during these six years, Nvidia stands ready to purchase compute at pre-agreed price levels that vary over the time period. Each Neocloud is expected to negotiate the backstop terms individually, and we can expect that different Neoclouds may end up with different revenue share and backstop schedules. The below table is an illustrative example to explain the basic terms of the program – we present a backstop pricing curve that we believe to be on the lower end of the backstop range – in this case at an average of $2.36 over the six-year period, but we expect that most Neoclouds will negotiate higher backstops.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e6\",\n    \"summary\": \"SharonAI announced in June 2026 a 72MW AI factory in Australia under a six-year Nvidia backstop, scaling to as many as 40,000 GB300s, with SharonAI disclosing a total backstop value of $4.88 billion, or an implied floor of about $2.33/hour/GPU on average over six years; SharonAI said its footprint would grow to 132MW with 102MW contracted and guided to more than 55,000 Nvidia GPUs in total, including the 40,000 backstopped GB300s, by mid-2027.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"The first is SharonAI’s 72MW AI factory in Australia. Announced in June 2026, the deal has SharonAI scaling up to as many as 40,000 GB300s under a six-year backstop. Sharon disclosed a total backstop value of $4.88B, which works out to an implied floor of about $2.33/hr/GPU on average over the six year period. Sharon AI’s footprint is now set to grow to 132MW with 102MW contracted, and the company guides to reaching more than 55,000 Nvidia GPUs in total (inclusive of the 40,000 GB300s backstopped) by mid-2027.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e7\",\n    \"summary\": \"Firmus announced on June 29, 2026 a 360MW AI cluster in Batam, Indonesia under an Nvidia backstop, likely to be housed in a DayOne facility in Kabil Industrial Tech Park; Firmus expects $25 billion to $30 billion of customer revenue over the six years, and SemiAnalysis expects a portion of revenue above the backstop to be shared with Nvidia.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Firmus’s 360MW AI Cluster in Batam, Indonesia is the next major project announced with an Nvidia backstop. It will likely be housed in a DayOne facility in Kabil Industrial Tech Park (KITP). Announced just recently on June 29 th , 2026, this project shows the scale at which Nvidia is rolling out the backstop program.\",\n     \"The Nvidia backstop allows Firmus to grow their business by an order of magnitude. As per Nvidia’s key objectives that we outlined above, the focus of this cluster will be to open up compute to a variety of AI Natives, Enterprises and Inference Providers, with many different rental tenors to be offered. Firmus announced that they expect $25B to $30B of customer revenue over the six years. We expect a portion of the revenue above the backstop level will be shared with Nvidia as per the structure we outlined earlier today.\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"point\": 2,\n  \"decisive_item\": {\n   \"day\": \"2026-07-06\",\n   \"title\": \"Nvidia GPU Debt Backstop Unleashes the AI Project Trinity: Capital, Offtake and Datacenters\",\n   \"who\": \"Firmus (announced June 29, 2026; reported by SemiAnalysis)\",\n   \"passage\": \"Firmus’s 360MW AI Cluster in Batam, Indonesia is the next major project announced with an Nvidia backstop.\",\n   \"meaning\": {\n    \"who\": \"Firmus\",\n    \"what\": \"a second announced NVIDIA-backstopped cluster\",\n    \"how_much\": \"360MW\",\n    \"period\": \"announced June 29, 2026\",\n    \"status\": \"happened\",\n    \"status_note\": \"announced\"\n   }\n  },\n  \"kept_from_that_article\": [\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e1\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis said that, three years into the AI buildout, the general market's Nvidia shipment-volume and revenue estimates for the second half of 2026 remain materially below its own supply-chain tracking in its Accelerator Model, even as Nvidia revenue is rising sharply.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Nvidia revenue is skyrocketing, and even 3 years into the build out, the general market is still materially lower on shipment volumes and revenue estimates for Nvidia in the 2nd half of this year versus our through supply chain tracking in the Accelerator Model .\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e2\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis forecast that AI debt financing will grow to more than $7 trillion outstanding by 2029, making it the second-largest asset-backed debt market after the roughly $13 trillion US mortgage-backed market, and that annual AI capex will exceed $2 trillion in 2028 with cumulative 2024–2029 AI capex of about $11.1 trillion, mainly funded by credit markets.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"AI Debt Financing will become a multi-trillion-dollar credit market, with over $7T of debt outstanding by 2029 driven both by AI IT Capex and AI Datacenter Capex needs for Neoclouds, datacenter builders, and even hyperscalers. This will make it the second largest asset backed debt market after the US mortgage-backed financing market at just over $13T.\\nAnnual AI Capex – including GPUs, networking, storage and attached CPU compute as well as for the Datacenters to house AI compute – will be well north of $2T in 2028. Cumulative AI Capex from 2024 to 2029 will reach ~$11.1T, and credit markets will be the main funding source for this buildout.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e3\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis reported that Nvidia has begun backstopping GPU rental offtakes, providing Neoclouds a take-or-pay commitment and minimum revenue guarantee on underlying GPU capacity while taking a share of Neocloud revenue earned above the backstop level.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"This is why Nvidia has stepped in and has started backstopping GPU rental offtakes themselves . In the backstop program, Nvidia provides a take-or-pay commitment to Neoclouds – a minimum revenue guarantee on the underlying GPU capacity. In exchange for this backstop, Nvidia also shares in a portion of the Neocloud’s revenue earned above the backstop level.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e4\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis said Nvidia has gone beyond GPU offtake backstops and has started backstopping datacenter leases as well, to help Neoclouds secure datacenter capacity.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Datacenter: However, even with the backstop in place, securing the final leg of the Trinity and obtaining datacenter capacity remains challenging absent a creative datacenter rental structure or resorting to a self-built datacenter. Here, Nvidia is diving even deeper, as it has started backstopping datacenter leases.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e5\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis described Nvidia's backstop program as typically six years, with Nvidia standing ready to buy compute at pre-agreed price levels that vary over time and each Neocloud negotiating individual revenue-share and backstop schedules; it presented an illustrative lower-end backstop pricing curve averaging $2.36 per GPU-hour over six years and said it expects most Neoclouds to negotiate higher backstops.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Nvidia’s backstop program is typically six years in length, and during these six years, Nvidia stands ready to purchase compute at pre-agreed price levels that vary over the time period. Each Neocloud is expected to negotiate the backstop terms individually, and we can expect that different Neoclouds may end up with different revenue share and backstop schedules. The below table is an illustrative example to explain the basic terms of the program – we present a backstop pricing curve that we believe to be on the lower end of the backstop range – in this case at an average of $2.36 over the six-year period, but we expect that most Neoclouds will negotiate higher backstops.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e6\",\n    \"summary\": \"SharonAI announced in June 2026 a 72MW AI factory in Australia under a six-year Nvidia backstop, scaling to as many as 40,000 GB300s, with SharonAI disclosing a total backstop value of $4.88 billion, or an implied floor of about $2.33/hour/GPU on average over six years; SharonAI said its footprint would grow to 132MW with 102MW contracted and guided to more than 55,000 Nvidia GPUs in total, including the 40,000 backstopped GB300s, by mid-2027.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"The first is SharonAI’s 72MW AI factory in Australia. Announced in June 2026, the deal has SharonAI scaling up to as many as 40,000 GB300s under a six-year backstop. Sharon disclosed a total backstop value of $4.88B, which works out to an implied floor of about $2.33/hr/GPU on average over the six year period. Sharon AI’s footprint is now set to grow to 132MW with 102MW contracted, and the company guides to reaching more than 55,000 Nvidia GPUs in total (inclusive of the 40,000 GB300s backstopped) by mid-2027.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e7\",\n    \"summary\": \"Firmus announced on June 29, 2026 a 360MW AI cluster in Batam, Indonesia under an Nvidia backstop, likely to be housed in a DayOne facility in Kabil Industrial Tech Park; Firmus expects $25 billion to $30 billion of customer revenue over the six years, and SemiAnalysis expects a portion of revenue above the backstop to be shared with Nvidia.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Firmus’s 360MW AI Cluster in Batam, Indonesia is the next major project announced with an Nvidia backstop. It will likely be housed in a DayOne facility in Kabil Industrial Tech Park (KITP). Announced just recently on June 29 th , 2026, this project shows the scale at which Nvidia is rolling out the backstop program.\",\n     \"The Nvidia backstop allows Firmus to grow their business by an order of magnitude. As per Nvidia’s key objectives that we outlined above, the focus of this cluster will be to open up compute to a variety of AI Natives, Enterprises and Inference Providers, with many different rental tenors to be offered. Firmus announced that they expect $25B to $30B of customer revenue over the six years. We expect a portion of the revenue above the backstop level will be shared with Nvidia as per the structure we outlined earlier today.\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"point\": 3,\n  \"decisive_item\": {\n   \"day\": \"2026-07-06\",\n   \"title\": \"Nvidia GPU Debt Backstop Unleashes the AI Project Trinity: Capital, Offtake and Datacenters\",\n   \"who\": \"Firmus\",\n   \"passage\": \"Firmus announced that they expect $25B to $30B of customer revenue over the six years\",\n   \"meaning\": {\n    \"who\": \"Firmus\",\n    \"what\": \"expected customer revenue on the backstopped Batam cluster (part above the backstop is shared with NVIDIA)\",\n    \"how_much\": \"$25B-$30B\",\n    \"period\": \"over six years\",\n    \"status\": \"forecast\"\n   }\n  },\n  \"kept_from_that_article\": [\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e1\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis said that, three years into the AI buildout, the general market's Nvidia shipment-volume and revenue estimates for the second half of 2026 remain materially below its own supply-chain tracking in its Accelerator Model, even as Nvidia revenue is rising sharply.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Nvidia revenue is skyrocketing, and even 3 years into the build out, the general market is still materially lower on shipment volumes and revenue estimates for Nvidia in the 2nd half of this year versus our through supply chain tracking in the Accelerator Model .\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e2\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis forecast that AI debt financing will grow to more than $7 trillion outstanding by 2029, making it the second-largest asset-backed debt market after the roughly $13 trillion US mortgage-backed market, and that annual AI capex will exceed $2 trillion in 2028 with cumulative 2024–2029 AI capex of about $11.1 trillion, mainly funded by credit markets.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"AI Debt Financing will become a multi-trillion-dollar credit market, with over $7T of debt outstanding by 2029 driven both by AI IT Capex and AI Datacenter Capex needs for Neoclouds, datacenter builders, and even hyperscalers. This will make it the second largest asset backed debt market after the US mortgage-backed financing market at just over $13T.\\nAnnual AI Capex – including GPUs, networking, storage and attached CPU compute as well as for the Datacenters to house AI compute – will be well north of $2T in 2028. Cumulative AI Capex from 2024 to 2029 will reach ~$11.1T, and credit markets will be the main funding source for this buildout.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e3\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis reported that Nvidia has begun backstopping GPU rental offtakes, providing Neoclouds a take-or-pay commitment and minimum revenue guarantee on underlying GPU capacity while taking a share of Neocloud revenue earned above the backstop level.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"This is why Nvidia has stepped in and has started backstopping GPU rental offtakes themselves . In the backstop program, Nvidia provides a take-or-pay commitment to Neoclouds – a minimum revenue guarantee on the underlying GPU capacity. In exchange for this backstop, Nvidia also shares in a portion of the Neocloud’s revenue earned above the backstop level.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e4\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis said Nvidia has gone beyond GPU offtake backstops and has started backstopping datacenter leases as well, to help Neoclouds secure datacenter capacity.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Datacenter: However, even with the backstop in place, securing the final leg of the Trinity and obtaining datacenter capacity remains challenging absent a creative datacenter rental structure or resorting to a self-built datacenter. Here, Nvidia is diving even deeper, as it has started backstopping datacenter leases.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e5\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis described Nvidia's backstop program as typically six years, with Nvidia standing ready to buy compute at pre-agreed price levels that vary over time and each Neocloud negotiating individual revenue-share and backstop schedules; it presented an illustrative lower-end backstop pricing curve averaging $2.36 per GPU-hour over six years and said it expects most Neoclouds to negotiate higher backstops.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Nvidia’s backstop program is typically six years in length, and during these six years, Nvidia stands ready to purchase compute at pre-agreed price levels that vary over the time period. Each Neocloud is expected to negotiate the backstop terms individually, and we can expect that different Neoclouds may end up with different revenue share and backstop schedules. The below table is an illustrative example to explain the basic terms of the program – we present a backstop pricing curve that we believe to be on the lower end of the backstop range – in this case at an average of $2.36 over the six-year period, but we expect that most Neoclouds will negotiate higher backstops.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e6\",\n    \"summary\": \"SharonAI announced in June 2026 a 72MW AI factory in Australia under a six-year Nvidia backstop, scaling to as many as 40,000 GB300s, with SharonAI disclosing a total backstop value of $4.88 billion, or an implied floor of about $2.33/hour/GPU on average over six years; SharonAI said its footprint would grow to 132MW with 102MW contracted and guided to more than 55,000 Nvidia GPUs in total, including the 40,000 backstopped GB300s, by mid-2027.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"The first is SharonAI’s 72MW AI factory in Australia. Announced in June 2026, the deal has SharonAI scaling up to as many as 40,000 GB300s under a six-year backstop. Sharon disclosed a total backstop value of $4.88B, which works out to an implied floor of about $2.33/hr/GPU on average over the six year period. Sharon AI’s footprint is now set to grow to 132MW with 102MW contracted, and the company guides to reaching more than 55,000 Nvidia GPUs in total (inclusive of the 40,000 GB300s backstopped) by mid-2027.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e7\",\n    \"summary\": \"Firmus announced on June 29, 2026 a 360MW AI cluster in Batam, Indonesia under an Nvidia backstop, likely to be housed in a DayOne facility in Kabil Industrial Tech Park; Firmus expects $25 billion to $30 billion of customer revenue over the six years, and SemiAnalysis expects a portion of revenue above the backstop to be shared with Nvidia.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Firmus’s 360MW AI Cluster in Batam, Indonesia is the next major project announced with an Nvidia backstop. It will likely be housed in a DayOne facility in Kabil Industrial Tech Park (KITP). Announced just recently on June 29 th , 2026, this project shows the scale at which Nvidia is rolling out the backstop program.\",\n     \"The Nvidia backstop allows Firmus to grow their business by an order of magnitude. As per Nvidia’s key objectives that we outlined above, the focus of this cluster will be to open up compute to a variety of AI Natives, Enterprises and Inference Providers, with many different rental tenors to be offered. Firmus announced that they expect $25B to $30B of customer revenue over the six years. We expect a portion of the revenue above the backstop level will be shared with Nvidia as per the structure we outlined earlier today.\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"point\": 4,\n  \"decisive_item\": {\n   \"day\": \"2026-07-06\",\n   \"title\": \"Nvidia GPU Debt Backstop Unleashes the AI Project Trinity: Capital, Offtake and Datacenters\",\n   \"who\": \"SemiAnalysis\",\n   \"passage\": \"Nvidia’s backstop program is only getting started, and we see announcements coming faster every quarter. Many Neoclouds are already deep in negotiations with Nvidia\",\n   \"meaning\": {\n    \"who\": \"SemiAnalysis\",\n    \"what\": \"more NVIDIA backstops exist but are not public; many neoclouds in negotiation; announcements accelerating\",\n    \"status\": \"claim\",\n    \"status_note\": \"existing undisclosed backstops; more deals are a forecast\"\n   }\n  },\n  \"kept_from_that_article\": [\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e1\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis said that, three years into the AI buildout, the general market's Nvidia shipment-volume and revenue estimates for the second half of 2026 remain materially below its own supply-chain tracking in its Accelerator Model, even as Nvidia revenue is rising sharply.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Nvidia revenue is skyrocketing, and even 3 years into the build out, the general market is still materially lower on shipment volumes and revenue estimates for Nvidia in the 2nd half of this year versus our through supply chain tracking in the Accelerator Model .\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e2\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis forecast that AI debt financing will grow to more than $7 trillion outstanding by 2029, making it the second-largest asset-backed debt market after the roughly $13 trillion US mortgage-backed market, and that annual AI capex will exceed $2 trillion in 2028 with cumulative 2024–2029 AI capex of about $11.1 trillion, mainly funded by credit markets.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"AI Debt Financing will become a multi-trillion-dollar credit market, with over $7T of debt outstanding by 2029 driven both by AI IT Capex and AI Datacenter Capex needs for Neoclouds, datacenter builders, and even hyperscalers. This will make it the second largest asset backed debt market after the US mortgage-backed financing market at just over $13T.\\nAnnual AI Capex – including GPUs, networking, storage and attached CPU compute as well as for the Datacenters to house AI compute – will be well north of $2T in 2028. Cumulative AI Capex from 2024 to 2029 will reach ~$11.1T, and credit markets will be the main funding source for this buildout.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e3\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis reported that Nvidia has begun backstopping GPU rental offtakes, providing Neoclouds a take-or-pay commitment and minimum revenue guarantee on underlying GPU capacity while taking a share of Neocloud revenue earned above the backstop level.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"This is why Nvidia has stepped in and has started backstopping GPU rental offtakes themselves . In the backstop program, Nvidia provides a take-or-pay commitment to Neoclouds – a minimum revenue guarantee on the underlying GPU capacity. In exchange for this backstop, Nvidia also shares in a portion of the Neocloud’s revenue earned above the backstop level.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e4\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis said Nvidia has gone beyond GPU offtake backstops and has started backstopping datacenter leases as well, to help Neoclouds secure datacenter capacity.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Datacenter: However, even with the backstop in place, securing the final leg of the Trinity and obtaining datacenter capacity remains challenging absent a creative datacenter rental structure or resorting to a self-built datacenter. Here, Nvidia is diving even deeper, as it has started backstopping datacenter leases.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e5\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis described Nvidia's backstop program as typically six years, with Nvidia standing ready to buy compute at pre-agreed price levels that vary over time and each Neocloud negotiating individual revenue-share and backstop schedules; it presented an illustrative lower-end backstop pricing curve averaging $2.36 per GPU-hour over six years and said it expects most Neoclouds to negotiate higher backstops.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Nvidia’s backstop program is typically six years in length, and during these six years, Nvidia stands ready to purchase compute at pre-agreed price levels that vary over the time period. Each Neocloud is expected to negotiate the backstop terms individually, and we can expect that different Neoclouds may end up with different revenue share and backstop schedules. The below table is an illustrative example to explain the basic terms of the program – we present a backstop pricing curve that we believe to be on the lower end of the backstop range – in this case at an average of $2.36 over the six-year period, but we expect that most Neoclouds will negotiate higher backstops.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e6\",\n    \"summary\": \"SharonAI announced in June 2026 a 72MW AI factory in Australia under a six-year Nvidia backstop, scaling to as many as 40,000 GB300s, with SharonAI disclosing a total backstop value of $4.88 billion, or an implied floor of about $2.33/hour/GPU on average over six years; SharonAI said its footprint would grow to 132MW with 102MW contracted and guided to more than 55,000 Nvidia GPUs in total, including the 40,000 backstopped GB300s, by mid-2027.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"The first is SharonAI’s 72MW AI factory in Australia. Announced in June 2026, the deal has SharonAI scaling up to as many as 40,000 GB300s under a six-year backstop. Sharon disclosed a total backstop value of $4.88B, which works out to an implied floor of about $2.33/hr/GPU on average over the six year period. Sharon AI’s footprint is now set to grow to 132MW with 102MW contracted, and the company guides to reaching more than 55,000 Nvidia GPUs in total (inclusive of the 40,000 GB300s backstopped) by mid-2027.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e7\",\n    \"summary\": \"Firmus announced on June 29, 2026 a 360MW AI cluster in Batam, Indonesia under an Nvidia backstop, likely to be housed in a DayOne facility in Kabil Industrial Tech Park; Firmus expects $25 billion to $30 billion of customer revenue over the six years, and SemiAnalysis expects a portion of revenue above the backstop to be shared with Nvidia.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Firmus’s 360MW AI Cluster in Batam, Indonesia is the next major project announced with an Nvidia backstop. It will likely be housed in a DayOne facility in Kabil Industrial Tech Park (KITP). Announced just recently on June 29 th , 2026, this project shows the scale at which Nvidia is rolling out the backstop program.\",\n     \"The Nvidia backstop allows Firmus to grow their business by an order of magnitude. As per Nvidia’s key objectives that we outlined above, the focus of this cluster will be to open up compute to a variety of AI Natives, Enterprises and Inference Providers, with many different rental tenors to be offered. Firmus announced that they expect $25B to $30B of customer revenue over the six years. We expect a portion of the revenue above the backstop level will be shared with Nvidia as per the structure we outlined earlier today.\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"point\": 5,\n  \"decisive_item\": {\n   \"day\": \"2026-07-01\",\n   \"title\": \"NVIDIA is about to start taking a share of cloud providers' revenue.\",\n   \"who\": \"The Information (prior reporting)\",\n   \"passage\": \"NVIDIA has also recently been negotiating to provide financial guarantees for OpenAI’s lease of a large data center in Ohio\",\n   \"meaning\": {\n    \"who\": \"NVIDIA (per The Information)\",\n    \"what\": \"negotiating to guarantee OpenAI's lease of a large Ohio data center\",\n    \"how_much\": \"the data center could cost as much as $500 billion if fully built (asset cost, not the guarantee amount)\",\n    \"condition\": \"in negotiation; nothing signed\",\n    \"status\": \"rumour\"\n   }\n  },\n  \"kept_from_that_article\": [\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:4d1677af37befd53ca3e7b45#ev1\",\n    \"summary\": \"The Information reported that NVIDIA is providing financial guarantees to young GPU cloud service providers that lease or rent its GPUs: if those companies fail to secure enough AI developers to rent the capacity, NVIDIA will repurchase their unsold GPU capacity at a pre-agreed price, and in return NVIDIA receives a percentage of the cloud providers' revenue that declines over the contract term. GPU cloud providers Firmus and Sharon AI have joined the program, and three executives with existing ties to NVIDIA confirmed the arrangement, per The Information.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"On July 1, according to the technology news outlet The Information, NVIDIA is providing financial guarantees to young cloud service providers that lease or rent its GPUs—if these companies fail to secure enough AI developers to rent their computing capacity, NVIDIA will repurchase their unsold GPU capacity at a pre-agreed price.\",\n     \"In return, NVIDIA will receive a percentage of these cloud providers’ revenue, with the revenue-sharing rate gradually declining over the term of the contract. GPU cloud providers Firmus and Sharon AI have already joined the program, and three executives with existing business ties to NVIDIA confirmed the arrangement.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:4d1677af37befd53ca3e7b45#ev2\",\n    \"summary\": \"On July 1, NVIDIA published a statement on its official website announcing a new business model combining revenue sharing with credit support, enabling AI cloud providers to procure NVIDIA infrastructure without bearing the full upfront capital expenditure, and to offer computing services to downstream AI-native enterprises, model developers and corporate clients. Per NVIDIA's official press release, the company will earn not only standard product revenue but also an additional share of cloud service revenue, creating a usage-based, recurring revenue stream.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"On July 1, NVIDIA published a statement on its official website announcing a new business model that combines revenue sharing with credit support, enabling AI cloud providers to procure NVIDIA infrastructure without bearing the full upfront capital expenditure, and allowing them to offer computing services to downstream AI-native enterprises, model developers, and corporate clients.\",\n     \"According to NVIDIA’s official press release, the company will earn not only standard product revenue but also an additional share of cloud service revenue, creating a usage-based, recurring revenue stream.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:4d1677af37befd53ca3e7b45#ev3\",\n    \"summary\": \"Sharon AI and Firmus are the first cloud providers participating in NVIDIA's new revenue-sharing/credit-support model. Sharon AI plans to deploy up to 40,000 NVIDIA Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs, while Firmus is constructing a DSX AI Factory campus in Batam, Indonesia, expected to scale up to 360 megawatts and accommodate up to 170,000 NVIDIA GPUs.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Sharon AI and Firmus are the first cloud providers participating in this model. Sharon AI plans to deploy up to 40,000 NVIDIA Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs, while Firmus is constructing a DSX AI Factory campus in Batam, Indonesia, expected to scale up to 360 megawatts and accommodate up to 170,000 NVIDIA GPUs.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:4d1677af37befd53ca3e7b45#ev4\",\n    \"summary\": \"According to prior reporting by The Information, NVIDIA has recently been negotiating to provide financial guarantees for OpenAI's lease of a large data center in Ohio—an asset that, if fully built out at current prices for chips, labor, power and other materials, could cost as much as $500 billion.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"According to prior reporting by The Information, NVIDIA has also recently been negotiating to provide financial guarantees for OpenAI’s lease of a large data center in Ohio—an asset that, if fully built out at current prices for chips, labor, power, and other materials, could cost as much as $500 billion.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:4d1677af37befd53ca3e7b45#ev5\",\n    \"summary\": \"In September 2024, NVIDIA committed to purchasing all of CoreWeave's unsold capacity through 2032 if CoreWeave failed to secure tenants, under a contract then valued at $6.3 billion.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"In September 2024, NVIDIA committed to purchasing all of CoreWeave’s unsold capacity through 2032 if CoreWeave failed to secure tenants, under a contract then valued at $6.3 billion.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:4d1677af37befd53ca3e7b45#ev6\",\n    \"summary\": \"According to a regulatory filing NVIDIA submitted in May of this year (covering the quarter ended in April), NVIDIA subsequently added another $3.5 billion to guarantee customer data center leases in exchange for rights to purchase their shares.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"According to a regulatory filing submitted by NVIDIA in May of this year (covering the quarter ended in April), NVIDIA subsequently added another $3.5 billion to guarantee customer data center leases in exchange for rights to purchase their shares.\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"point\": 6,\n  \"decisive_item\": {\n   \"day\": \"2026-07-07\",\n   \"title\": \"Nvidia Stock Is Down 17% From Its High. Is the Artificial Intelligence (AI) Leader Finally Cheap?\",\n   \"who\": \"the big four hyperscalers (2026 plans, summarised by the author); NVIDIA (2027 claim)\",\n   \"passage\": \"This year, the big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data center capital expenditures . Next year, Nvidia claims this group will spend more than $1 trillion.\",\n   \"meaning\": {\n    \"who\": \"big four AI hyperscalers' own plans (2026); NVIDIA's claim (2027)\",\n    \"what\": \"data-center capital expenditure of the big four\",\n    \"how_much\": \"around $650bn (2026); more than $1 trillion (2027)\",\n    \"period\": \"calendar 2026; calendar 2027\",\n    \"status\": \"plan (2026); forecast by NVIDIA (2027)\"\n   }\n  },\n  \"kept_from_that_article\": [\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:f8191f5bb960d79e47c73d6c#e1\",\n    \"summary\": \"The article reports that the 'big four' AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year (calendar 2026).\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"This year, the big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data center capital expenditures .\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:f8191f5bb960d79e47c73d6c#e2\",\n    \"summary\": \"Nvidia claims the 'big four' AI hyperscalers will spend more than $1 trillion on data center capital expenditures next year (calendar 2027).\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Next year, Nvidia claims this group will spend more than $1 trillion.\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"point\": 7,\n  \"decisive_item\": {\n   \"day\": \"2026-07-04\",\n   \"title\": \"Nvidia Stock Is Nearly Flat for 2026. Time to Cash Out, or Load Up on Shares?\",\n   \"who\": \"Wall Street analysts (consensus, as reported by the author)\",\n   \"passage\": \"Wall Street analysts project 82% revenue growth for this year and 41% next year\",\n   \"meaning\": {\n    \"who\": \"Wall Street analysts\",\n    \"what\": \"NVIDIA revenue growth forecast\",\n    \"how_much\": \"82% this year; 41% next year\",\n    \"period\": \"this year and next year (fiscal or calendar both accepted, D233)\",\n    \"status\": \"forecast\"\n   }\n  },\n  \"kept_from_that_article\": [\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:92c7e54d38dc2916aa185568#ev-1\",\n    \"summary\": \"According to fool.com, the big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year (calendar 2026).\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"The big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:92c7e54d38dc2916aa185568#ev-2\",\n    \"summary\": \"Nvidia projects, as reported by fool.com, that hyperscaler data center capital expenditures could reach $1 trillion next year (calendar 2027).\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Next year, Nvidia projects that this figure could reach $1 trillion.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:92c7e54d38dc2916aa185568#ev-3\",\n    \"summary\": \"fool.com reports that Wall Street analysts project 82% revenue growth for Nvidia this year (calendar 2026) and 41% revenue growth next year (calendar 2027).\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Wall Street analysts project 82% revenue growth for this year and 41% next year, so there's another external source that's projecting even more growth for Nvidia.\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"point\": 8,\n  \"decisive_item\": {\n   \"day\": \"2026-07-06\",\n   \"title\": \"How Nvidia Became The Black Sheep Of The Chip Stock Rally\",\n   \"who\": \"unnamed estimates\",\n   \"passage\": \"Some estimates put half of all data center spend going toward memory in 2027.\",\n   \"meaning\": {\n    \"who\": \"unnamed estimates\",\n    \"what\": \"memory's share of data center spend\",\n    \"how_much\": \"about half\",\n    \"period\": \"2027\",\n    \"status\": \"forecast\"\n   }\n  },\n  \"kept_from_that_article\": [\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:3df0bbc8aff2c1d008bc0a32#ev-revenue\",\n    \"summary\": \"NVIDIA reported revenue rose 85% year over year to $81.6 billion, with data center revenue up 92% to $75.2 billion.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Revenue rose 85% year over year to $81.6 billion, with data center up 92% to $75.2 billion.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:3df0bbc8aff2c1d008bc0a32#ev-supply-commit\",\n    \"summary\": \"NVIDIA carries $119 billion in supply commitments, which the article says hinge on AI delivering measurable economic value beyond productivity tools.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Nvidia's $119B in supply commitments hinge on AI delivering measurable economic value beyond productivity tools, which is a question the market appears to already be pricing in.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:3df0bbc8aff2c1d008bc0a32#ev-vera-rubin-delay\",\n    \"summary\": \"NVIDIA shares came under pressure on reports of delays across its upcoming lineup of Vera Rubin server systems.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"NVIDIA shares are under pressure on reports of delays across their upcoming lineup of Vera Rubin server systems.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:3df0bbc8aff2c1d008bc0a32#ev-broadcom-ai-guidance\",\n    \"summary\": \"Broadcom guided Q3 AI semiconductor revenue to $16.0 billion, more than 200% year over year.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Broadcom guided Q3 AI semiconductor revenue to $16.0 billion, more than 200% year over year.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:3df0bbc8aff2c1d008bc0a32#ev-bloomberg-asic-cagr\",\n    \"summary\": \"Bloomberg Intelligence forecasts a 27% compound annual growth rate for custom ASICs through 2033, versus 16% for AI accelerators.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Broadcom's custom ASICs for Alphabet and Meta are eating the story, with Bloomberg Intelligence forecasting a 27% CAGR for custom ASICs through 2033 versus 16% for AI accelerators.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:3df0bbc8aff2c1d008bc0a32#ev-amd-meta-instinct\",\n    \"summary\": \"AMD's Meta Instinct GPU deal anchors a multi-year hyperscaler thesis, and AMD CEO Lisa Su said customer forecasts for MI450 are exceeding the company's initial expectations.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"AMD’s Meta Instinct GPU deal anchors a multi-year hyperscaler thesis , and Lisa Su noted “leading customer forecasts exceeding our initial expectations” on MI450.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:3df0bbc8aff2c1d008bc0a32#ev-micron-revenue\",\n    \"summary\": \"Micron reported Q3 revenue up 345.7% year over year to $41.46 billion, and CEO Sanjay Mehrotra pointed to multi-year Strategic Customer Agreements designed to lock in durability.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Q3 revenue exploded 345.7% year over year to $41.46 billion, and CEO Sanjay Mehrotra pointed to multi-year Strategic Customer Agreements designed to lock in durability.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:3df0bbc8aff2c1d008bc0a32#ev-micron-margin\",\n    \"summary\": \"Micron is forecasting adjusted gross margins of 86% next quarter, above the peak gross margins NVIDIA ever achieved.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Most strikingly, Micron is now forecasting adjusted gross margins of 86% next quarter, above the peak gross margins NVIDIA ever achieved.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:3df0bbc8aff2c1d008bc0a32#ev-dc-memory-share\",\n    \"summary\": \"Unnamed estimates put half of all data center spending going toward memory in 2027.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Some estimates put half of all data center spend going toward memory in 2027.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:3df0bbc8aff2c1d008bc0a32#ev-intel-stake\",\n    \"summary\": \"Intel's share surge is attributed in part to NVIDIA's own $5 billion equity investment in Intel.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Intel ( NASDAQ:INTC ), still deep in a turnaround under Lip-Bu Tan, has surged 278.4% on DCAI momentum and NVIDIA’s own $5 billion equity investment.\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"point\": 9,\n  \"decisive_item\": {\n   \"day\": \"2026-07-06\",\n   \"title\": \"CAPITAL IDEAS: The lag 7 — Is Micron the new Nvidia?\",\n   \"who\": \"Goldman Sachs (as cited)\",\n   \"passage\": \"Goldman Sachs estimates annual AI infrastructure capital expenditures could reach $765 billion in 2026 and grow to $1.6 trillion by 2031\",\n   \"meaning\": {\n    \"who\": \"Goldman Sachs estimate\",\n    \"what\": \"annual AI infrastructure capex (chips, data centers, power, cooling, networking)\",\n    \"how_much\": \"$765B (2026); $1.6T (2031); ~$7.6T cumulative 2026-2031\",\n    \"period\": \"2026; 2031; 2026-2031\",\n    \"condition\": \"could reach\",\n    \"status\": \"forecast\"\n   }\n  },\n  \"kept_from_that_article\": [\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:54aa9ce326115cedf8857512#ev-1\",\n    \"summary\": \"Micron Technology reported revenue of $41.46 billion in its third fiscal quarter of 2026, versus $9.30 billion a year earlier (roughly 346% growth), GAAP net income of $28.24 billion versus $1.89 billion a year earlier, gross margin of 84.6% versus 37.7%, and operating margin of 80.4% versus 23.3%. As a memory supplier, Micron's reported revenue and margin surge is a read on memory/HBM pricing and supply conditions that set NVIDIA's memory input costs.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"For its third fiscal quarter of 2026, Micron reported revenue of $41.46 billion. One year earlier, revenue was $9.30 billion. That is roughly 346 percent revenue growth. GAAP (generally accepted accounting principles) net income was $28.24 billion, compared with $1.89 billion a year ago. Micron’s gross margin was 84.6 percent, up from 37.7 percent a year earlier, and its operating margin was 80.4 percent, up from 23.3 percent.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:54aa9ce326115cedf8857512#ev-2\",\n    \"summary\": \"The columnist reports NVIDIA's latest revenue growth at about 85%, GAAP gross margin at 74.9% and operating margin at roughly 66%, comparing each with Micron's 346% growth, 84.6% gross margin and 80.4% operating margin; the item does not state which NVIDIA quarter these figures cover, and says NVIDIA still earns more dollars.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Nvidia remains the king of AI chips. Micron’s latest revenue growth was about 346 percent, compared with Nvidia’s 85 percent. Micron’s GAAP gross margin was 84.6 percent, compared with Nvidia’s 74.9 percent. Micron’s operating margin was 80.4 percent, compared with Nvidia’s roughly 66 percent. Nvidia still earns more dollars.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:54aa9ce326115cedf8857512#ev-3\",\n    \"summary\": \"Apple said it can no longer absorb the surge in memory and storage costs and announced price increases for some Macs and iPads, citing higher memory costs — a device maker passing memory-cost inflation into end-product prices.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Apple said it can no longer absorb the surge in memory and storage costs and announced price increases for some Macs and iPads, citing higher memory costs.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:54aa9ce326115cedf8857512#ev-4\",\n    \"summary\": \"Goldman Sachs estimates annual AI infrastructure capital expenditures could reach $765 billion in 2026 and grow to $1.6 trillion by 2031, for cumulative spending of about $7.6 trillion from 2026 through 2031, covering chips, data centers, power, cooling and networking.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Goldman Sachs estimates annual AI infrastructure capital expenditures could reach $765 billion in 2026 and grow to $1.6 trillion by 2031, with cumulative spending of about $7.6 trillion from 2026 through 2031. That spending includes chips, data centers, power, cooling, networking, and the other plumbing required to run AI at scale.\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"point\": 10,\n  \"decisive_item\": {\n   \"day\": \"2026-07-05\",\n   \"title\": \"Nvidia's next-gen AI rack system delayed to 2028 on manufacturing snags, SemiAnalysis says\",\n   \"who\": \"SemiAnalysis (post on Monday)\",\n   \"passage\": \"Kyber NVL144 rack architecture has been delayed to 2028 as the PCB midplane remains challenging from a manufacturability standpoint\",\n   \"meaning\": {\n    \"who\": \"SemiAnalysis (a research firm's report, not NVIDIA)\",\n    \"what\": \"Kyber NVL144 rack for Vera Rubin Ultra delayed; cause: PCB midplane manufacturability\",\n    \"how_much\": \"more than 12 months\",\n    \"period\": \"from 2027 to 2028\",\n    \"status\": \"claim\"\n   }\n  },\n  \"kept_from_that_article\": [\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:552f8c2bdfd080555d592587#E1\",\n    \"summary\": \"Research firm SemiAnalysis reported that Nvidia's Kyber rack-scale architecture (Kyber NVL144), designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips and slated to debut with Vera Rubin Ultra in 2027, has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028, because the multi-layer PCB midplane at the heart of the system remains difficult to manufacture.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Nvidia 's next marquee product — the Kyber rack-scale architecture designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips — has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028, according to research firm SemiAnalysis, the latest in a string of reported setbacks raising questions about the AI giant's product roadmap.\",\n     \"The design mounts graphics processing units in compute trays that sit vertically instead of horizontally to boost density and reduce latency, and had been slated to debut with Vera Rubin Ultra, Nvidia's next-generation rack-scale system, in 2027 .\",\n     \"The setback stems from difficulties manufacturing a key circuit board at the heart of the system, SemiAnalysis said in a post on Monday.\",\n     \"\\\"Kyber NVL144 rack architecture has been delayed to 2028 as the PCB midplane remains challenging from a manufacturability standpoint,\\\" the firm said, referring to a specialized, multi-layer printed circuit board that connects electronic modules within a system.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:552f8c2bdfd080555d592587#E2\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis also said that Nvidia's NVL576, a larger system linking eight racks via optical connections, is likely to be delayed or limited to small volumes.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"NVL576 — a larger system linking eight racks via optical connections — is also likely delayed or limited to small volumes, the research firm said.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:552f8c2bdfd080555d592587#E3\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis reported that Nvidia's backup plan — bolting two of its current-generation racks together to reach similar power — has been scrapped after cloud service providers and hyperscalers pushed back on its awkward design and heavy operational burden.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"A backup plan — bolting two of Nvidia's current-generation racks together for similar power — has also been scrapped after cloud customers rejected the design as awkward and costly to operate.\",\n     \"\\\"It has since been cancelled due to heavy pushback from CSPs [cloud service providers] and hyperscalers over its odd design and heavy operational burden,\\\" SemiAnalysis said.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:552f8c2bdfd080555d592587#E4\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis said that, with the backup design cancelled, Nvidia has \\\"no proven solution to expand the scale-up world size for Rubin Ultra,\\\" and predicted that this could give rivals Advanced Micro Devices and Google — whose in-house chips are already winning business from top AI labs — a rare technical opening at the high end of the market.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"That leaves Nvidia with \\\"no proven solution to expand the scale-up world size for Rubin Ultra,\\\" SemiAnalysis said, predicting that could give rivals Advanced Micro Devices and Google , whose in-house chips are already winning business from top AI labs, a rare technical opening at the high end of the market.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:552f8c2bdfd080555d592587#E5\",\n    \"summary\": \"Nvidia rejected SemiAnalysis's report of the Kyber delay, stating that \\\"Our roadmap is intact.\\\"\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Nvidia rejected the SemiAnalysis report and said, \\\"Our roadmap is intact.\\\"\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:552f8c2bdfd080555d592587#E6\",\n    \"summary\": \"Nvidia's current-generation Rubin systems are in full production and are to begin shipping this fall (fall 2026) to eight cloud partners, including Amazon Web Services, Microsoft Azure and Google Cloud.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Nvidia's current-generation Rubin systems are in full production and begin shipping this fall to eight cloud partners, including Amazon Web Services, Microsoft Azure and Google Cloud.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:552f8c2bdfd080555d592587#E7\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis projects that Nvidia's data-center compute revenue will run 20% above Wall Street consensus in the second half of fiscal 2027.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"SemiAnalysis also projects Nvidia's data-center compute revenue will run 20% above Wall Street consensus in the second half of fiscal 2027.\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"point\": 11,\n  \"decisive_item\": {\n   \"day\": \"2026-07-06\",\n   \"title\": \"Nvidia's Kyber NVL144 reportedly pushed back more than a year, Asian suppliers drop\",\n   \"who\": \"SemiAnalysis (as reported)\",\n   \"passage\": \"The more powerful version of the upcoming Rubin Ultra chip with four compute dies has also been canceled. Only the smaller two-die version remains, delivering roughly half the real-world compute performance.\",\n   \"meaning\": {\n    \"who\": \"SemiAnalysis\",\n    \"what\": \"four-die Rubin Ultra cancelled; two-die version remains\",\n    \"how_much\": \"roughly half the real-world compute\",\n    \"status\": \"claim\",\n    \"status_note\": \"cancelled\"\n   }\n  },\n  \"kept_from_that_article\": [\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:795ea94e1100415a67fd1a96#ev-1\",\n    \"summary\": \"Analyst firm SemiAnalysis reported on X that Nvidia's next-generation AI server rack, Kyber NVL144, has been pushed back more than twelve months to 2028 because of manufacturing problems — specifically the PCB midplane, a central circuit board connecting the rack's components, proving extremely difficult to produce without defects. Jensen Huang had shown off Kyber NVL144 three months earlier at GTC.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Asian tech stocks slid after analyst firm SemiAnalysis reported on X that Nvidia's next AI server rack system, Kyber NVL144, has been pushed back more than twelve months to 2028 because of manufacturing problems.\",\n     \"The issue, according to SemiAnalysis, is the circuit boards. Specifically, the PCB midplane, a central board that connects all the individual components, has proven extremely difficult to produce without defects. Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang had shown off Kyber NVL144 just three months earlier at the company's GTC conference.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:795ea94e1100415a67fd1a96#ev-2\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis reported that Nvidia scrapped its planned NVL72x2 rack design entirely; the design would have placed two Oberon racks back to back, and cloud providers and large data center operators pushed back against the unusual form factor and high operational overhead.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"A planned alternative design called NVL72x2, which would have placed two Oberon racks back to back, has been scrapped entirely. Cloud providers and large data center operators pushed back against the unusual form factor and the high operational overhead.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:795ea94e1100415a67fd1a96#ev-3\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis reported that Nvidia canceled the more powerful four-compute-die version of its upcoming Rubin Ultra chip; only the smaller two-die version remains, delivering roughly half the real-world compute performance.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"The more powerful version of the upcoming Rubin Ultra chip with four compute dies has also been canceled. Only the smaller two-die version remains, delivering roughly half the real-world compute performance.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:795ea94e1100415a67fd1a96#ev-4\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis reported that Nvidia's CPO-NVSwitch interconnect technology, which links many chips into a single large system, won't arrive until the generation after next (Feynman), meaning Nvidia lacks a proven way to scale Rubin Ultra to very large systems for now; the gap could give competitors such as AMD's MI500X or Google's TPUv8i Broadfly room to move in, and Nvidia plans to make up for the shortfall by selling more Oberon-Rubin racks in the existing form factor.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"A key interconnect technology called CPO-NVSwitch, which links many chips into a single large system, won't arrive until the generation after next, called Feynman. That means Nvidia lacks a proven way to scale Rubin Ultra to very large systems for now. The gap could give competitors like AMD's MI500X or Google's TPUv8i Broadfly room to move in. Nvidia plans to make up for the shortfall by selling more Oberon-Rubin racks in the existing form factor.\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"point\": 12,\n  \"decisive_item\": {\n   \"day\": \"2026-07-06\",\n   \"title\": \"Nvidia's Kyber NVL144 reportedly pushed back more than a year, Asian suppliers drop\",\n   \"who\": \"SemiAnalysis (as reported)\",\n   \"passage\": \"Nvidia plans to make up for the shortfall by selling more Oberon-Rubin racks in the existing form factor.\",\n   \"meaning\": {\n    \"who\": \"SemiAnalysis, about NVIDIA's plan\",\n    \"what\": \"sell more Oberon-Rubin racks to cover the Kyber gap\",\n    \"status\": \"plan\",\n    \"status_note\": \"as claimed\"\n   }\n  },\n  \"kept_from_that_article\": [\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:795ea94e1100415a67fd1a96#ev-1\",\n    \"summary\": \"Analyst firm SemiAnalysis reported on X that Nvidia's next-generation AI server rack, Kyber NVL144, has been pushed back more than twelve months to 2028 because of manufacturing problems — specifically the PCB midplane, a central circuit board connecting the rack's components, proving extremely difficult to produce without defects. Jensen Huang had shown off Kyber NVL144 three months earlier at GTC.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Asian tech stocks slid after analyst firm SemiAnalysis reported on X that Nvidia's next AI server rack system, Kyber NVL144, has been pushed back more than twelve months to 2028 because of manufacturing problems.\",\n     \"The issue, according to SemiAnalysis, is the circuit boards. Specifically, the PCB midplane, a central board that connects all the individual components, has proven extremely difficult to produce without defects. Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang had shown off Kyber NVL144 just three months earlier at the company's GTC conference.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:795ea94e1100415a67fd1a96#ev-2\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis reported that Nvidia scrapped its planned NVL72x2 rack design entirely; the design would have placed two Oberon racks back to back, and cloud providers and large data center operators pushed back against the unusual form factor and high operational overhead.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"A planned alternative design called NVL72x2, which would have placed two Oberon racks back to back, has been scrapped entirely. Cloud providers and large data center operators pushed back against the unusual form factor and the high operational overhead.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:795ea94e1100415a67fd1a96#ev-3\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis reported that Nvidia canceled the more powerful four-compute-die version of its upcoming Rubin Ultra chip; only the smaller two-die version remains, delivering roughly half the real-world compute performance.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"The more powerful version of the upcoming Rubin Ultra chip with four compute dies has also been canceled. Only the smaller two-die version remains, delivering roughly half the real-world compute performance.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:795ea94e1100415a67fd1a96#ev-4\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis reported that Nvidia's CPO-NVSwitch interconnect technology, which links many chips into a single large system, won't arrive until the generation after next (Feynman), meaning Nvidia lacks a proven way to scale Rubin Ultra to very large systems for now; the gap could give competitors such as AMD's MI500X or Google's TPUv8i Broadfly room to move in, and Nvidia plans to make up for the shortfall by selling more Oberon-Rubin racks in the existing form factor.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"A key interconnect technology called CPO-NVSwitch, which links many chips into a single large system, won't arrive until the generation after next, called Feynman. That means Nvidia lacks a proven way to scale Rubin Ultra to very large systems for now. The gap could give competitors like AMD's MI500X or Google's TPUv8i Broadfly room to move in. Nvidia plans to make up for the shortfall by selling more Oberon-Rubin racks in the existing form factor.\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"point\": 13,\n  \"decisive_item\": {\n   \"day\": \"2026-07-05\",\n   \"title\": \"Nvidia's next-gen AI rack system delayed to 2028 on manufacturing snags, SemiAnalysis says\",\n   \"who\": \"NVIDIA (statement to CNBC)\",\n   \"passage\": \"Our roadmap is intact\",\n   \"meaning\": {\n    \"who\": \"NVIDIA\",\n    \"what\": \"rejects the SemiAnalysis report; says its roadmap (Rubin Ultra / Kyber in 2027) is unchanged\",\n    \"status\": \"claim\"\n   }\n  },\n  \"kept_from_that_article\": [\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:552f8c2bdfd080555d592587#E1\",\n    \"summary\": \"Research firm SemiAnalysis reported that Nvidia's Kyber rack-scale architecture (Kyber NVL144), designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips and slated to debut with Vera Rubin Ultra in 2027, has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028, because the multi-layer PCB midplane at the heart of the system remains difficult to manufacture.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Nvidia 's next marquee product — the Kyber rack-scale architecture designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips — has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028, according to research firm SemiAnalysis, the latest in a string of reported setbacks raising questions about the AI giant's product roadmap.\",\n     \"The design mounts graphics processing units in compute trays that sit vertically instead of horizontally to boost density and reduce latency, and had been slated to debut with Vera Rubin Ultra, Nvidia's next-generation rack-scale system, in 2027 .\",\n     \"The setback stems from difficulties manufacturing a key circuit board at the heart of the system, SemiAnalysis said in a post on Monday.\",\n     \"\\\"Kyber NVL144 rack architecture has been delayed to 2028 as the PCB midplane remains challenging from a manufacturability standpoint,\\\" the firm said, referring to a specialized, multi-layer printed circuit board that connects electronic modules within a system.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:552f8c2bdfd080555d592587#E2\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis also said that Nvidia's NVL576, a larger system linking eight racks via optical connections, is likely to be delayed or limited to small volumes.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"NVL576 — a larger system linking eight racks via optical connections — is also likely delayed or limited to small volumes, the research firm said.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:552f8c2bdfd080555d592587#E3\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis reported that Nvidia's backup plan — bolting two of its current-generation racks together to reach similar power — has been scrapped after cloud service providers and hyperscalers pushed back on its awkward design and heavy operational burden.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"A backup plan — bolting two of Nvidia's current-generation racks together for similar power — has also been scrapped after cloud customers rejected the design as awkward and costly to operate.\",\n     \"\\\"It has since been cancelled due to heavy pushback from CSPs [cloud service providers] and hyperscalers over its odd design and heavy operational burden,\\\" SemiAnalysis said.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:552f8c2bdfd080555d592587#E4\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis said that, with the backup design cancelled, Nvidia has \\\"no proven solution to expand the scale-up world size for Rubin Ultra,\\\" and predicted that this could give rivals Advanced Micro Devices and Google — whose in-house chips are already winning business from top AI labs — a rare technical opening at the high end of the market.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"That leaves Nvidia with \\\"no proven solution to expand the scale-up world size for Rubin Ultra,\\\" SemiAnalysis said, predicting that could give rivals Advanced Micro Devices and Google , whose in-house chips are already winning business from top AI labs, a rare technical opening at the high end of the market.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:552f8c2bdfd080555d592587#E5\",\n    \"summary\": \"Nvidia rejected SemiAnalysis's report of the Kyber delay, stating that \\\"Our roadmap is intact.\\\"\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Nvidia rejected the SemiAnalysis report and said, \\\"Our roadmap is intact.\\\"\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:552f8c2bdfd080555d592587#E6\",\n    \"summary\": \"Nvidia's current-generation Rubin systems are in full production and are to begin shipping this fall (fall 2026) to eight cloud partners, including Amazon Web Services, Microsoft Azure and Google Cloud.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Nvidia's current-generation Rubin systems are in full production and begin shipping this fall to eight cloud partners, including Amazon Web Services, Microsoft Azure and Google Cloud.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:552f8c2bdfd080555d592587#E7\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis projects that Nvidia's data-center compute revenue will run 20% above Wall Street consensus in the second half of fiscal 2027.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"SemiAnalysis also projects Nvidia's data-center compute revenue will run 20% above Wall Street consensus in the second half of fiscal 2027.\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"point\": 14,\n  \"decisive_item\": {\n   \"day\": \"2026-07-06\",\n   \"title\": \"Nvidia's Kyber rack for Rubin Ultra reportedly delayed to 2028, stopgap solution also axed due to customer pushback — Analyst firm SemiAnalysis says PCB midplane problems led to the delay [Updated]\",\n   \"who\": \"NVIDIA representative\",\n   \"passage\": \"An Nvidia representative responded with a short statement to Tom's Hardware : \\\"Our roadmap is intact.\\\"\",\n   \"meaning\": {\n    \"who\": \"NVIDIA representative\",\n    \"what\": \"denies the report: roadmap intact\",\n    \"condition\": \"unclear whether the original or an already updated roadmap is meant\",\n    \"status\": \"claim\"\n   }\n  },\n  \"kept_from_that_article\": [\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:c75f16a6d64cc27fc28fb571#ev-1\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis reported on X that Nvidia will not ship its Kyber NVL144 rack until 2028, a delay of more than 12 months that pushes the cabinet intended for 2027's Rubin Ultra GPUs into the following year. The reported holdup is manufacturing challenges with a PCB midplane — Nvidia's 'orthogonal backplane' — that connects eight Oberon racks between the NVSwitches. Tom's Hardware relays the report as unconfirmed.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Nvidia reportedly won't ship its Kyber NVL144 rack until 2028, a delay of more than 12 months that pushes the cabinet meant for 2027's Rubin Ultra GPUs into the following year, according to a SemiAnalysis thread on X. The holdup is ostensibly being caused by manufacturing challenges with a PCB midplane that connects eight Oberon racks between the NVSwitches, which Nvidia calls the orthogonal backplane.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:c75f16a6d64cc27fc28fb571#ev-2\",\n    \"summary\": \"According to SemiAnalysis, Nvidia killed the NVL72x2 stopgap rack — which would have bolted two Oberon racks back-to-back to reach Kyber-class density over copper NVLink — after its largest customers balked at running two linked cabinets as a single unit. The report says this leaves no proven alternative to widen Rubin Ultra's scale-up in 2027.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Nvidia is also understood to have killed NVL72x2, a stopgap rack designed to tide customers over, and that no proven alternative is now available to widen Rubin Ultra's scale-up in 2027.\",\n     \"NVL72x2 would have bolted two Oberon racks back-to-back to reach Kyber-class density over copper NVLink, per SemiAnalysis , which said Nvidia abandoned the stopgap after its largest customers balked at running two linked cabinets as a single unit.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:c75f16a6d64cc27fc28fb571#ev-3\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis reported that Nvidia's NVL576 configuration — which ties eight racks together through co-packaged optics — is likely to slip or ship in low volume until that optical technology matures, and it placed a fully production-ready co-packaged-optics NVSwitch no earlier than the Feynman generation that follows Rubin, leaving copper as the near-term rack-scale interconnect.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"NVL576, a separate configuration tying eight racks together through co-packaged optics, is likely to slip too or ship in low volume until that optical technology matures.\",\n     \"SemiAnalysis has also placed a fully production-ready co-packaged optics NVSwitch no earlier than the Feynman generation that follows Rubin, which leaves copper as the only near-term solution for linking Rubin Ultra at rack scale and thereby puts even more weight on the PCB midplane.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:c75f16a6d64cc27fc28fb571#ev-4\",\n    \"summary\": \"The item reports that Nvidia dropped the quad-chiplet Rubin Ultra GPU in favour of a dual-chiplet part 'last week' over manufacturing execution concerns, halving the accelerator's per-package compute. Reported, not officially confirmed by Nvidia.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Nvidia dropped the quad-chiplet Rubin Ultra GPU for a dual-chiplet part last week over manufacturing execution concerns, halving the accelerator's per-package compute.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:c75f16a6d64cc27fc28fb571#ev-5\",\n    \"summary\": \"An Nvidia representative responded to Tom's Hardware with the short statement \\\"Our roadmap is intact,\\\" providing no further detail; the outlet notes it is unclear whether this refers to the original roadmap and delivery schedules or to an already-updated one.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"An Nvidia representative responded with a short statement to Tom's Hardware : \\\"Our roadmap is intact.\\\"\",\n     \"Nvidia provided no further details in response to our follow-up questions. As such, it is unclear if Nvidia's statement refers to its original roadmap, planned delivery schedules, and hardware, or if those particulars had already been changed and the statement refers to a newly updated roadmap.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:c75f16a6d64cc27fc28fb571#ev-6\",\n    \"summary\": \"The item reports that the Kyber delay applies only to the Rubin Ultra phase and its Kyber rack, and that Nvidia's 2026 Rubin GPUs, which reuse the current Oberon rack, are not part of the reported delay.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"The delay only applies only to the Rubin Ultra phase and its Kyber rack. Nvidia's 2026 Rubin GPUs, which reuse the current Oberon rack, aren't part of the reported delay.\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"point\": 15,\n  \"decisive_item\": {\n   \"day\": \"2026-07-01\",\n   \"title\": \"NVIDIA is about to start taking a share of cloud providers' revenue.\",\n   \"who\": \"NVIDIA (May 2026 quarterly filing)\",\n   \"passage\": \"NVIDIA subsequently added another $3.5 billion to guarantee customer data center leases in exchange for rights to purchase their shares\",\n   \"meaning\": {\n    \"who\": \"NVIDIA\",\n    \"what\": \"guarantees of customer data center leases, in exchange for rights to buy their shares\",\n    \"how_much\": \"an additional $3.5 billion\",\n    \"period\": \"quarter ended April 2026 (filed May)\",\n    \"status\": \"happened\"\n   }\n  },\n  \"kept_from_that_article\": [\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:4d1677af37befd53ca3e7b45#ev1\",\n    \"summary\": \"The Information reported that NVIDIA is providing financial guarantees to young GPU cloud service providers that lease or rent its GPUs: if those companies fail to secure enough AI developers to rent the capacity, NVIDIA will repurchase their unsold GPU capacity at a pre-agreed price, and in return NVIDIA receives a percentage of the cloud providers' revenue that declines over the contract term. GPU cloud providers Firmus and Sharon AI have joined the program, and three executives with existing ties to NVIDIA confirmed the arrangement, per The Information.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"On July 1, according to the technology news outlet The Information, NVIDIA is providing financial guarantees to young cloud service providers that lease or rent its GPUs—if these companies fail to secure enough AI developers to rent their computing capacity, NVIDIA will repurchase their unsold GPU capacity at a pre-agreed price.\",\n     \"In return, NVIDIA will receive a percentage of these cloud providers’ revenue, with the revenue-sharing rate gradually declining over the term of the contract. GPU cloud providers Firmus and Sharon AI have already joined the program, and three executives with existing business ties to NVIDIA confirmed the arrangement.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:4d1677af37befd53ca3e7b45#ev2\",\n    \"summary\": \"On July 1, NVIDIA published a statement on its official website announcing a new business model combining revenue sharing with credit support, enabling AI cloud providers to procure NVIDIA infrastructure without bearing the full upfront capital expenditure, and to offer computing services to downstream AI-native enterprises, model developers and corporate clients. Per NVIDIA's official press release, the company will earn not only standard product revenue but also an additional share of cloud service revenue, creating a usage-based, recurring revenue stream.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"On July 1, NVIDIA published a statement on its official website announcing a new business model that combines revenue sharing with credit support, enabling AI cloud providers to procure NVIDIA infrastructure without bearing the full upfront capital expenditure, and allowing them to offer computing services to downstream AI-native enterprises, model developers, and corporate clients.\",\n     \"According to NVIDIA’s official press release, the company will earn not only standard product revenue but also an additional share of cloud service revenue, creating a usage-based, recurring revenue stream.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:4d1677af37befd53ca3e7b45#ev3\",\n    \"summary\": \"Sharon AI and Firmus are the first cloud providers participating in NVIDIA's new revenue-sharing/credit-support model. Sharon AI plans to deploy up to 40,000 NVIDIA Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs, while Firmus is constructing a DSX AI Factory campus in Batam, Indonesia, expected to scale up to 360 megawatts and accommodate up to 170,000 NVIDIA GPUs.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Sharon AI and Firmus are the first cloud providers participating in this model. Sharon AI plans to deploy up to 40,000 NVIDIA Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs, while Firmus is constructing a DSX AI Factory campus in Batam, Indonesia, expected to scale up to 360 megawatts and accommodate up to 170,000 NVIDIA GPUs.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:4d1677af37befd53ca3e7b45#ev4\",\n    \"summary\": \"According to prior reporting by The Information, NVIDIA has recently been negotiating to provide financial guarantees for OpenAI's lease of a large data center in Ohio—an asset that, if fully built out at current prices for chips, labor, power and other materials, could cost as much as $500 billion.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"According to prior reporting by The Information, NVIDIA has also recently been negotiating to provide financial guarantees for OpenAI’s lease of a large data center in Ohio—an asset that, if fully built out at current prices for chips, labor, power, and other materials, could cost as much as $500 billion.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:4d1677af37befd53ca3e7b45#ev5\",\n    \"summary\": \"In September 2024, NVIDIA committed to purchasing all of CoreWeave's unsold capacity through 2032 if CoreWeave failed to secure tenants, under a contract then valued at $6.3 billion.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"In September 2024, NVIDIA committed to purchasing all of CoreWeave’s unsold capacity through 2032 if CoreWeave failed to secure tenants, under a contract then valued at $6.3 billion.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:4d1677af37befd53ca3e7b45#ev6\",\n    \"summary\": \"According to a regulatory filing NVIDIA submitted in May of this year (covering the quarter ended in April), NVIDIA subsequently added another $3.5 billion to guarantee customer data center leases in exchange for rights to purchase their shares.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"According to a regulatory filing submitted by NVIDIA in May of this year (covering the quarter ended in April), NVIDIA subsequently added another $3.5 billion to guarantee customer data center leases in exchange for rights to purchase their shares.\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"point\": 16,\n  \"decisive_item\": {\n   \"day\": \"2026-07-06\",\n   \"title\": \"Nvidia GPU Debt Backstop Unleashes the AI Project Trinity: Capital, Offtake and Datacenters\",\n   \"who\": \"SemiAnalysis\",\n   \"passage\": \"Nvidia is diving even deeper, as it has started backstopping datacenter leases\",\n   \"meaning\": {\n    \"who\": \"SemiAnalysis\",\n    \"what\": \"NVIDIA backstops datacenter leases for neoclouds (a second kind of obligation)\",\n    \"status\": \"happened\"\n   }\n  },\n  \"kept_from_that_article\": [\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e1\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis said that, three years into the AI buildout, the general market's Nvidia shipment-volume and revenue estimates for the second half of 2026 remain materially below its own supply-chain tracking in its Accelerator Model, even as Nvidia revenue is rising sharply.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Nvidia revenue is skyrocketing, and even 3 years into the build out, the general market is still materially lower on shipment volumes and revenue estimates for Nvidia in the 2nd half of this year versus our through supply chain tracking in the Accelerator Model .\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e2\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis forecast that AI debt financing will grow to more than $7 trillion outstanding by 2029, making it the second-largest asset-backed debt market after the roughly $13 trillion US mortgage-backed market, and that annual AI capex will exceed $2 trillion in 2028 with cumulative 2024–2029 AI capex of about $11.1 trillion, mainly funded by credit markets.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"AI Debt Financing will become a multi-trillion-dollar credit market, with over $7T of debt outstanding by 2029 driven both by AI IT Capex and AI Datacenter Capex needs for Neoclouds, datacenter builders, and even hyperscalers. This will make it the second largest asset backed debt market after the US mortgage-backed financing market at just over $13T.\\nAnnual AI Capex – including GPUs, networking, storage and attached CPU compute as well as for the Datacenters to house AI compute – will be well north of $2T in 2028. Cumulative AI Capex from 2024 to 2029 will reach ~$11.1T, and credit markets will be the main funding source for this buildout.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e3\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis reported that Nvidia has begun backstopping GPU rental offtakes, providing Neoclouds a take-or-pay commitment and minimum revenue guarantee on underlying GPU capacity while taking a share of Neocloud revenue earned above the backstop level.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"This is why Nvidia has stepped in and has started backstopping GPU rental offtakes themselves . In the backstop program, Nvidia provides a take-or-pay commitment to Neoclouds – a minimum revenue guarantee on the underlying GPU capacity. In exchange for this backstop, Nvidia also shares in a portion of the Neocloud’s revenue earned above the backstop level.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e4\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis said Nvidia has gone beyond GPU offtake backstops and has started backstopping datacenter leases as well, to help Neoclouds secure datacenter capacity.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Datacenter: However, even with the backstop in place, securing the final leg of the Trinity and obtaining datacenter capacity remains challenging absent a creative datacenter rental structure or resorting to a self-built datacenter. Here, Nvidia is diving even deeper, as it has started backstopping datacenter leases.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e5\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis described Nvidia's backstop program as typically six years, with Nvidia standing ready to buy compute at pre-agreed price levels that vary over time and each Neocloud negotiating individual revenue-share and backstop schedules; it presented an illustrative lower-end backstop pricing curve averaging $2.36 per GPU-hour over six years and said it expects most Neoclouds to negotiate higher backstops.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Nvidia’s backstop program is typically six years in length, and during these six years, Nvidia stands ready to purchase compute at pre-agreed price levels that vary over the time period. Each Neocloud is expected to negotiate the backstop terms individually, and we can expect that different Neoclouds may end up with different revenue share and backstop schedules. The below table is an illustrative example to explain the basic terms of the program – we present a backstop pricing curve that we believe to be on the lower end of the backstop range – in this case at an average of $2.36 over the six-year period, but we expect that most Neoclouds will negotiate higher backstops.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e6\",\n    \"summary\": \"SharonAI announced in June 2026 a 72MW AI factory in Australia under a six-year Nvidia backstop, scaling to as many as 40,000 GB300s, with SharonAI disclosing a total backstop value of $4.88 billion, or an implied floor of about $2.33/hour/GPU on average over six years; SharonAI said its footprint would grow to 132MW with 102MW contracted and guided to more than 55,000 Nvidia GPUs in total, including the 40,000 backstopped GB300s, by mid-2027.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"The first is SharonAI’s 72MW AI factory in Australia. Announced in June 2026, the deal has SharonAI scaling up to as many as 40,000 GB300s under a six-year backstop. Sharon disclosed a total backstop value of $4.88B, which works out to an implied floor of about $2.33/hr/GPU on average over the six year period. Sharon AI’s footprint is now set to grow to 132MW with 102MW contracted, and the company guides to reaching more than 55,000 Nvidia GPUs in total (inclusive of the 40,000 GB300s backstopped) by mid-2027.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e7\",\n    \"summary\": \"Firmus announced on June 29, 2026 a 360MW AI cluster in Batam, Indonesia under an Nvidia backstop, likely to be housed in a DayOne facility in Kabil Industrial Tech Park; Firmus expects $25 billion to $30 billion of customer revenue over the six years, and SemiAnalysis expects a portion of revenue above the backstop to be shared with Nvidia.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Firmus’s 360MW AI Cluster in Batam, Indonesia is the next major project announced with an Nvidia backstop. It will likely be housed in a DayOne facility in Kabil Industrial Tech Park (KITP). Announced just recently on June 29 th , 2026, this project shows the scale at which Nvidia is rolling out the backstop program.\",\n     \"The Nvidia backstop allows Firmus to grow their business by an order of magnitude. As per Nvidia’s key objectives that we outlined above, the focus of this cluster will be to open up compute to a variety of AI Natives, Enterprises and Inference Providers, with many different rental tenors to be offered. Firmus announced that they expect $25B to $30B of customer revenue over the six years. We expect a portion of the revenue above the backstop level will be shared with Nvidia as per the structure we outlined earlier today.\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ]\n }\n]\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json; p=json.load(open(\\\"/Users/lchingen/hivemind/clean-arch/e2e-comparison-2026-11-13/full-trace/trace-packet-r1.json\\\")); [print(json.dumps(x,ensure_ascii=False)) for x in p if 4 <= x[\\\"point\\\"] <= 13]'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json; p=json.load(open(\\\"/Users/lchingen/hivemind/clean-arch/e2e-comparison-2026-11-13/full-trace/trace-packet-r1.json\\\")); [print(json.dumps(x,ensure_ascii=False)) for x in p if 4 <= x[\\\"point\\\"] <= 13]'\"","aggregated_output":"{\"point\": 4, \"decisive_item\": {\"day\": \"2026-07-06\", \"title\": \"Nvidia GPU Debt Backstop Unleashes the AI Project Trinity: Capital, Offtake and Datacenters\", \"who\": \"SemiAnalysis\", \"passage\": \"Nvidia’s backstop program is only getting started, and we see announcements coming faster every quarter. Many Neoclouds are already deep in negotiations with Nvidia\", \"meaning\": {\"who\": \"SemiAnalysis\", \"what\": \"more NVIDIA backstops exist but are not public; many neoclouds in negotiation; announcements accelerating\", \"status\": \"claim\", \"status_note\": \"existing undisclosed backstops; more deals are a forecast\"}}, \"kept_from_that_article\": [{\"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e1\", \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis said that, three years into the AI buildout, the general market's Nvidia shipment-volume and revenue estimates for the second half of 2026 remain materially below its own supply-chain tracking in its Accelerator Model, even as Nvidia revenue is rising sharply.\", \"passages\": [\"Nvidia revenue is skyrocketing, and even 3 years into the build out, the general market is still materially lower on shipment volumes and revenue estimates for Nvidia in the 2nd half of this year versus our through supply chain tracking in the Accelerator Model .\"]}, {\"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e2\", \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis forecast that AI debt financing will grow to more than $7 trillion outstanding by 2029, making it the second-largest asset-backed debt market after the roughly $13 trillion US mortgage-backed market, and that annual AI capex will exceed $2 trillion in 2028 with cumulative 2024–2029 AI capex of about $11.1 trillion, mainly funded by credit markets.\", \"passages\": [\"AI Debt Financing will become a multi-trillion-dollar credit market, with over $7T of debt outstanding by 2029 driven both by AI IT Capex and AI Datacenter Capex needs for Neoclouds, datacenter builders, and even hyperscalers. This will make it the second largest asset backed debt market after the US mortgage-backed financing market at just over $13T.\\nAnnual AI Capex – including GPUs, networking, storage and attached CPU compute as well as for the Datacenters to house AI compute – will be well north of $2T in 2028. Cumulative AI Capex from 2024 to 2029 will reach ~$11.1T, and credit markets will be the main funding source for this buildout.\"]}, {\"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e3\", \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis reported that Nvidia has begun backstopping GPU rental offtakes, providing Neoclouds a take-or-pay commitment and minimum revenue guarantee on underlying GPU capacity while taking a share of Neocloud revenue earned above the backstop level.\", \"passages\": [\"This is why Nvidia has stepped in and has started backstopping GPU rental offtakes themselves . In the backstop program, Nvidia provides a take-or-pay commitment to Neoclouds – a minimum revenue guarantee on the underlying GPU capacity. In exchange for this backstop, Nvidia also shares in a portion of the Neocloud’s revenue earned above the backstop level.\"]}, {\"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e4\", \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis said Nvidia has gone beyond GPU offtake backstops and has started backstopping datacenter leases as well, to help Neoclouds secure datacenter capacity.\", \"passages\": [\"Datacenter: However, even with the backstop in place, securing the final leg of the Trinity and obtaining datacenter capacity remains challenging absent a creative datacenter rental structure or resorting to a self-built datacenter. Here, Nvidia is diving even deeper, as it has started backstopping datacenter leases.\"]}, {\"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e5\", \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis described Nvidia's backstop program as typically six years, with Nvidia standing ready to buy compute at pre-agreed price levels that vary over time and each Neocloud negotiating individual revenue-share and backstop schedules; it presented an illustrative lower-end backstop pricing curve averaging $2.36 per GPU-hour over six years and said it expects most Neoclouds to negotiate higher backstops.\", \"passages\": [\"Nvidia’s backstop program is typically six years in length, and during these six years, Nvidia stands ready to purchase compute at pre-agreed price levels that vary over the time period. Each Neocloud is expected to negotiate the backstop terms individually, and we can expect that different Neoclouds may end up with different revenue share and backstop schedules. The below table is an illustrative example to explain the basic terms of the program – we present a backstop pricing curve that we believe to be on the lower end of the backstop range – in this case at an average of $2.36 over the six-year period, but we expect that most Neoclouds will negotiate higher backstops.\"]}, {\"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e6\", \"summary\": \"SharonAI announced in June 2026 a 72MW AI factory in Australia under a six-year Nvidia backstop, scaling to as many as 40,000 GB300s, with SharonAI disclosing a total backstop value of $4.88 billion, or an implied floor of about $2.33/hour/GPU on average over six years; SharonAI said its footprint would grow to 132MW with 102MW contracted and guided to more than 55,000 Nvidia GPUs in total, including the 40,000 backstopped GB300s, by mid-2027.\", \"passages\": [\"The first is SharonAI’s 72MW AI factory in Australia. Announced in June 2026, the deal has SharonAI scaling up to as many as 40,000 GB300s under a six-year backstop. Sharon disclosed a total backstop value of $4.88B, which works out to an implied floor of about $2.33/hr/GPU on average over the six year period. Sharon AI’s footprint is now set to grow to 132MW with 102MW contracted, and the company guides to reaching more than 55,000 Nvidia GPUs in total (inclusive of the 40,000 GB300s backstopped) by mid-2027.\"]}, {\"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e7\", \"summary\": \"Firmus announced on June 29, 2026 a 360MW AI cluster in Batam, Indonesia under an Nvidia backstop, likely to be housed in a DayOne facility in Kabil Industrial Tech Park; Firmus expects $25 billion to $30 billion of customer revenue over the six years, and SemiAnalysis expects a portion of revenue above the backstop to be shared with Nvidia.\", \"passages\": [\"Firmus’s 360MW AI Cluster in Batam, Indonesia is the next major project announced with an Nvidia backstop. It will likely be housed in a DayOne facility in Kabil Industrial Tech Park (KITP). Announced just recently on June 29 th , 2026, this project shows the scale at which Nvidia is rolling out the backstop program.\", \"The Nvidia backstop allows Firmus to grow their business by an order of magnitude. As per Nvidia’s key objectives that we outlined above, the focus of this cluster will be to open up compute to a variety of AI Natives, Enterprises and Inference Providers, with many different rental tenors to be offered. Firmus announced that they expect $25B to $30B of customer revenue over the six years. We expect a portion of the revenue above the backstop level will be shared with Nvidia as per the structure we outlined earlier today.\"]}]}\n{\"point\": 5, \"decisive_item\": {\"day\": \"2026-07-01\", \"title\": \"NVIDIA is about to start taking a share of cloud providers' revenue.\", \"who\": \"The Information (prior reporting)\", \"passage\": \"NVIDIA has also recently been negotiating to provide financial guarantees for OpenAI’s lease of a large data center in Ohio\", \"meaning\": {\"who\": \"NVIDIA (per The Information)\", \"what\": \"negotiating to guarantee OpenAI's lease of a large Ohio data center\", \"how_much\": \"the data center could cost as much as $500 billion if fully built (asset cost, not the guarantee amount)\", \"condition\": \"in negotiation; nothing signed\", \"status\": \"rumour\"}}, \"kept_from_that_article\": [{\"ref\": \"gnews:4d1677af37befd53ca3e7b45#ev1\", \"summary\": \"The Information reported that NVIDIA is providing financial guarantees to young GPU cloud service providers that lease or rent its GPUs: if those companies fail to secure enough AI developers to rent the capacity, NVIDIA will repurchase their unsold GPU capacity at a pre-agreed price, and in return NVIDIA receives a percentage of the cloud providers' revenue that declines over the contract term. GPU cloud providers Firmus and Sharon AI have joined the program, and three executives with existing ties to NVIDIA confirmed the arrangement, per The Information.\", \"passages\": [\"On July 1, according to the technology news outlet The Information, NVIDIA is providing financial guarantees to young cloud service providers that lease or rent its GPUs—if these companies fail to secure enough AI developers to rent their computing capacity, NVIDIA will repurchase their unsold GPU capacity at a pre-agreed price.\", \"In return, NVIDIA will receive a percentage of these cloud providers’ revenue, with the revenue-sharing rate gradually declining over the term of the contract. GPU cloud providers Firmus and Sharon AI have already joined the program, and three executives with existing business ties to NVIDIA confirmed the arrangement.\"]}, {\"ref\": \"gnews:4d1677af37befd53ca3e7b45#ev2\", \"summary\": \"On July 1, NVIDIA published a statement on its official website announcing a new business model combining revenue sharing with credit support, enabling AI cloud providers to procure NVIDIA infrastructure without bearing the full upfront capital expenditure, and to offer computing services to downstream AI-native enterprises, model developers and corporate clients. Per NVIDIA's official press release, the company will earn not only standard product revenue but also an additional share of cloud service revenue, creating a usage-based, recurring revenue stream.\", \"passages\": [\"On July 1, NVIDIA published a statement on its official website announcing a new business model that combines revenue sharing with credit support, enabling AI cloud providers to procure NVIDIA infrastructure without bearing the full upfront capital expenditure, and allowing them to offer computing services to downstream AI-native enterprises, model developers, and corporate clients.\", \"According to NVIDIA’s official press release, the company will earn not only standard product revenue but also an additional share of cloud service revenue, creating a usage-based, recurring revenue stream.\"]}, {\"ref\": \"gnews:4d1677af37befd53ca3e7b45#ev3\", \"summary\": \"Sharon AI and Firmus are the first cloud providers participating in NVIDIA's new revenue-sharing/credit-support model. Sharon AI plans to deploy up to 40,000 NVIDIA Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs, while Firmus is constructing a DSX AI Factory campus in Batam, Indonesia, expected to scale up to 360 megawatts and accommodate up to 170,000 NVIDIA GPUs.\", \"passages\": [\"Sharon AI and Firmus are the first cloud providers participating in this model. Sharon AI plans to deploy up to 40,000 NVIDIA Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs, while Firmus is constructing a DSX AI Factory campus in Batam, Indonesia, expected to scale up to 360 megawatts and accommodate up to 170,000 NVIDIA GPUs.\"]}, {\"ref\": \"gnews:4d1677af37befd53ca3e7b45#ev4\", \"summary\": \"According to prior reporting by The Information, NVIDIA has recently been negotiating to provide financial guarantees for OpenAI's lease of a large data center in Ohio—an asset that, if fully built out at current prices for chips, labor, power and other materials, could cost as much as $500 billion.\", \"passages\": [\"According to prior reporting by The Information, NVIDIA has also recently been negotiating to provide financial guarantees for OpenAI’s lease of a large data center in Ohio—an asset that, if fully built out at current prices for chips, labor, power, and other materials, could cost as much as $500 billion.\"]}, {\"ref\": \"gnews:4d1677af37befd53ca3e7b45#ev5\", \"summary\": \"In September 2024, NVIDIA committed to purchasing all of CoreWeave's unsold capacity through 2032 if CoreWeave failed to secure tenants, under a contract then valued at $6.3 billion.\", \"passages\": [\"In September 2024, NVIDIA committed to purchasing all of CoreWeave’s unsold capacity through 2032 if CoreWeave failed to secure tenants, under a contract then valued at $6.3 billion.\"]}, {\"ref\": \"gnews:4d1677af37befd53ca3e7b45#ev6\", \"summary\": \"According to a regulatory filing NVIDIA submitted in May of this year (covering the quarter ended in April), NVIDIA subsequently added another $3.5 billion to guarantee customer data center leases in exchange for rights to purchase their shares.\", \"passages\": [\"According to a regulatory filing submitted by NVIDIA in May of this year (covering the quarter ended in April), NVIDIA subsequently added another $3.5 billion to guarantee customer data center leases in exchange for rights to purchase their shares.\"]}]}\n{\"point\": 6, \"decisive_item\": {\"day\": \"2026-07-07\", \"title\": \"Nvidia Stock Is Down 17% From Its High. Is the Artificial Intelligence (AI) Leader Finally Cheap?\", \"who\": \"the big four hyperscalers (2026 plans, summarised by the author); NVIDIA (2027 claim)\", \"passage\": \"This year, the big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data center capital expenditures . Next year, Nvidia claims this group will spend more than $1 trillion.\", \"meaning\": {\"who\": \"big four AI hyperscalers' own plans (2026); NVIDIA's claim (2027)\", \"what\": \"data-center capital expenditure of the big four\", \"how_much\": \"around $650bn (2026); more than $1 trillion (2027)\", \"period\": \"calendar 2026; calendar 2027\", \"status\": \"plan (2026); forecast by NVIDIA (2027)\"}}, \"kept_from_that_article\": [{\"ref\": \"gnews:f8191f5bb960d79e47c73d6c#e1\", \"summary\": \"The article reports that the 'big four' AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year (calendar 2026).\", \"passages\": [\"This year, the big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data center capital expenditures .\"]}, {\"ref\": \"gnews:f8191f5bb960d79e47c73d6c#e2\", \"summary\": \"Nvidia claims the 'big four' AI hyperscalers will spend more than $1 trillion on data center capital expenditures next year (calendar 2027).\", \"passages\": [\"Next year, Nvidia claims this group will spend more than $1 trillion.\"]}]}\n{\"point\": 7, \"decisive_item\": {\"day\": \"2026-07-04\", \"title\": \"Nvidia Stock Is Nearly Flat for 2026. Time to Cash Out, or Load Up on Shares?\", \"who\": \"Wall Street analysts (consensus, as reported by the author)\", \"passage\": \"Wall Street analysts project 82% revenue growth for this year and 41% next year\", \"meaning\": {\"who\": \"Wall Street analysts\", \"what\": \"NVIDIA revenue growth forecast\", \"how_much\": \"82% this year; 41% next year\", \"period\": \"this year and next year (fiscal or calendar both accepted, D233)\", \"status\": \"forecast\"}}, \"kept_from_that_article\": [{\"ref\": \"gnews:92c7e54d38dc2916aa185568#ev-1\", \"summary\": \"According to fool.com, the big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year (calendar 2026).\", \"passages\": [\"The big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year.\"]}, {\"ref\": \"gnews:92c7e54d38dc2916aa185568#ev-2\", \"summary\": \"Nvidia projects, as reported by fool.com, that hyperscaler data center capital expenditures could reach $1 trillion next year (calendar 2027).\", \"passages\": [\"Next year, Nvidia projects that this figure could reach $1 trillion.\"]}, {\"ref\": \"gnews:92c7e54d38dc2916aa185568#ev-3\", \"summary\": \"fool.com reports that Wall Street analysts project 82% revenue growth for Nvidia this year (calendar 2026) and 41% revenue growth next year (calendar 2027).\", \"passages\": [\"Wall Street analysts project 82% revenue growth for this year and 41% next year, so there's another external source that's projecting even more growth for Nvidia.\"]}]}\n{\"point\": 8, \"decisive_item\": {\"day\": \"2026-07-06\", \"title\": \"How Nvidia Became The Black Sheep Of The Chip Stock Rally\", \"who\": \"unnamed estimates\", \"passage\": \"Some estimates put half of all data center spend going toward memory in 2027.\", \"meaning\": {\"who\": \"unnamed estimates\", \"what\": \"memory's share of data center spend\", \"how_much\": \"about half\", \"period\": \"2027\", \"status\": \"forecast\"}}, \"kept_from_that_article\": [{\"ref\": \"gnews:3df0bbc8aff2c1d008bc0a32#ev-revenue\", \"summary\": \"NVIDIA reported revenue rose 85% year over year to $81.6 billion, with data center revenue up 92% to $75.2 billion.\", \"passages\": [\"Revenue rose 85% year over year to $81.6 billion, with data center up 92% to $75.2 billion.\"]}, {\"ref\": \"gnews:3df0bbc8aff2c1d008bc0a32#ev-supply-commit\", \"summary\": \"NVIDIA carries $119 billion in supply commitments, which the article says hinge on AI delivering measurable economic value beyond productivity tools.\", \"passages\": [\"Nvidia's $119B in supply commitments hinge on AI delivering measurable economic value beyond productivity tools, which is a question the market appears to already be pricing in.\"]}, {\"ref\": \"gnews:3df0bbc8aff2c1d008bc0a32#ev-vera-rubin-delay\", \"summary\": \"NVIDIA shares came under pressure on reports of delays across its upcoming lineup of Vera Rubin server systems.\", \"passages\": [\"NVIDIA shares are under pressure on reports of delays across their upcoming lineup of Vera Rubin server systems.\"]}, {\"ref\": \"gnews:3df0bbc8aff2c1d008bc0a32#ev-broadcom-ai-guidance\", \"summary\": \"Broadcom guided Q3 AI semiconductor revenue to $16.0 billion, more than 200% year over year.\", \"passages\": [\"Broadcom guided Q3 AI semiconductor revenue to $16.0 billion, more than 200% year over year.\"]}, {\"ref\": \"gnews:3df0bbc8aff2c1d008bc0a32#ev-bloomberg-asic-cagr\", \"summary\": \"Bloomberg Intelligence forecasts a 27% compound annual growth rate for custom ASICs through 2033, versus 16% for AI accelerators.\", \"passages\": [\"Broadcom's custom ASICs for Alphabet and Meta are eating the story, with Bloomberg Intelligence forecasting a 27% CAGR for custom ASICs through 2033 versus 16% for AI accelerators.\"]}, {\"ref\": \"gnews:3df0bbc8aff2c1d008bc0a32#ev-amd-meta-instinct\", \"summary\": \"AMD's Meta Instinct GPU deal anchors a multi-year hyperscaler thesis, and AMD CEO Lisa Su said customer forecasts for MI450 are exceeding the company's initial expectations.\", \"passages\": [\"AMD’s Meta Instinct GPU deal anchors a multi-year hyperscaler thesis , and Lisa Su noted “leading customer forecasts exceeding our initial expectations” on MI450.\"]}, {\"ref\": \"gnews:3df0bbc8aff2c1d008bc0a32#ev-micron-revenue\", \"summary\": \"Micron reported Q3 revenue up 345.7% year over year to $41.46 billion, and CEO Sanjay Mehrotra pointed to multi-year Strategic Customer Agreements designed to lock in durability.\", \"passages\": [\"Q3 revenue exploded 345.7% year over year to $41.46 billion, and CEO Sanjay Mehrotra pointed to multi-year Strategic Customer Agreements designed to lock in durability.\"]}, {\"ref\": \"gnews:3df0bbc8aff2c1d008bc0a32#ev-micron-margin\", \"summary\": \"Micron is forecasting adjusted gross margins of 86% next quarter, above the peak gross margins NVIDIA ever achieved.\", \"passages\": [\"Most strikingly, Micron is now forecasting adjusted gross margins of 86% next quarter, above the peak gross margins NVIDIA ever achieved.\"]}, {\"ref\": \"gnews:3df0bbc8aff2c1d008bc0a32#ev-dc-memory-share\", \"summary\": \"Unnamed estimates put half of all data center spending going toward memory in 2027.\", \"passages\": [\"Some estimates put half of all data center spend going toward memory in 2027.\"]}, {\"ref\": \"gnews:3df0bbc8aff2c1d008bc0a32#ev-intel-stake\", \"summary\": \"Intel's share surge is attributed in part to NVIDIA's own $5 billion equity investment in Intel.\", \"passages\": [\"Intel ( NASDAQ:INTC ), still deep in a turnaround under Lip-Bu Tan, has surged 278.4% on DCAI momentum and NVIDIA’s own $5 billion equity investment.\"]}]}\n{\"point\": 9, \"decisive_item\": {\"day\": \"2026-07-06\", \"title\": \"CAPITAL IDEAS: The lag 7 — Is Micron the new Nvidia?\", \"who\": \"Goldman Sachs (as cited)\", \"passage\": \"Goldman Sachs estimates annual AI infrastructure capital expenditures could reach $765 billion in 2026 and grow to $1.6 trillion by 2031\", \"meaning\": {\"who\": \"Goldman Sachs estimate\", \"what\": \"annual AI infrastructure capex (chips, data centers, power, cooling, networking)\", \"how_much\": \"$765B (2026); $1.6T (2031); ~$7.6T cumulative 2026-2031\", \"period\": \"2026; 2031; 2026-2031\", \"condition\": \"could reach\", \"status\": \"forecast\"}}, \"kept_from_that_article\": [{\"ref\": \"gnews:54aa9ce326115cedf8857512#ev-1\", \"summary\": \"Micron Technology reported revenue of $41.46 billion in its third fiscal quarter of 2026, versus $9.30 billion a year earlier (roughly 346% growth), GAAP net income of $28.24 billion versus $1.89 billion a year earlier, gross margin of 84.6% versus 37.7%, and operating margin of 80.4% versus 23.3%. As a memory supplier, Micron's reported revenue and margin surge is a read on memory/HBM pricing and supply conditions that set NVIDIA's memory input costs.\", \"passages\": [\"For its third fiscal quarter of 2026, Micron reported revenue of $41.46 billion. One year earlier, revenue was $9.30 billion. That is roughly 346 percent revenue growth. GAAP (generally accepted accounting principles) net income was $28.24 billion, compared with $1.89 billion a year ago. Micron’s gross margin was 84.6 percent, up from 37.7 percent a year earlier, and its operating margin was 80.4 percent, up from 23.3 percent.\"]}, {\"ref\": \"gnews:54aa9ce326115cedf8857512#ev-2\", \"summary\": \"The columnist reports NVIDIA's latest revenue growth at about 85%, GAAP gross margin at 74.9% and operating margin at roughly 66%, comparing each with Micron's 346% growth, 84.6% gross margin and 80.4% operating margin; the item does not state which NVIDIA quarter these figures cover, and says NVIDIA still earns more dollars.\", \"passages\": [\"Nvidia remains the king of AI chips. Micron’s latest revenue growth was about 346 percent, compared with Nvidia’s 85 percent. Micron’s GAAP gross margin was 84.6 percent, compared with Nvidia’s 74.9 percent. Micron’s operating margin was 80.4 percent, compared with Nvidia’s roughly 66 percent. Nvidia still earns more dollars.\"]}, {\"ref\": \"gnews:54aa9ce326115cedf8857512#ev-3\", \"summary\": \"Apple said it can no longer absorb the surge in memory and storage costs and announced price increases for some Macs and iPads, citing higher memory costs — a device maker passing memory-cost inflation into end-product prices.\", \"passages\": [\"Apple said it can no longer absorb the surge in memory and storage costs and announced price increases for some Macs and iPads, citing higher memory costs.\"]}, {\"ref\": \"gnews:54aa9ce326115cedf8857512#ev-4\", \"summary\": \"Goldman Sachs estimates annual AI infrastructure capital expenditures could reach $765 billion in 2026 and grow to $1.6 trillion by 2031, for cumulative spending of about $7.6 trillion from 2026 through 2031, covering chips, data centers, power, cooling and networking.\", \"passages\": [\"Goldman Sachs estimates annual AI infrastructure capital expenditures could reach $765 billion in 2026 and grow to $1.6 trillion by 2031, with cumulative spending of about $7.6 trillion from 2026 through 2031. That spending includes chips, data centers, power, cooling, networking, and the other plumbing required to run AI at scale.\"]}]}\n{\"point\": 10, \"decisive_item\": {\"day\": \"2026-07-05\", \"title\": \"Nvidia's next-gen AI rack system delayed to 2028 on manufacturing snags, SemiAnalysis says\", \"who\": \"SemiAnalysis (post on Monday)\", \"passage\": \"Kyber NVL144 rack architecture has been delayed to 2028 as the PCB midplane remains challenging from a manufacturability standpoint\", \"meaning\": {\"who\": \"SemiAnalysis (a research firm's report, not NVIDIA)\", \"what\": \"Kyber NVL144 rack for Vera Rubin Ultra delayed; cause: PCB midplane manufacturability\", \"how_much\": \"more than 12 months\", \"period\": \"from 2027 to 2028\", \"status\": \"claim\"}}, \"kept_from_that_article\": [{\"ref\": \"gnews:552f8c2bdfd080555d592587#E1\", \"summary\": \"Research firm SemiAnalysis reported that Nvidia's Kyber rack-scale architecture (Kyber NVL144), designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips and slated to debut with Vera Rubin Ultra in 2027, has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028, because the multi-layer PCB midplane at the heart of the system remains difficult to manufacture.\", \"passages\": [\"Nvidia 's next marquee product — the Kyber rack-scale architecture designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips — has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028, according to research firm SemiAnalysis, the latest in a string of reported setbacks raising questions about the AI giant's product roadmap.\", \"The design mounts graphics processing units in compute trays that sit vertically instead of horizontally to boost density and reduce latency, and had been slated to debut with Vera Rubin Ultra, Nvidia's next-generation rack-scale system, in 2027 .\", \"The setback stems from difficulties manufacturing a key circuit board at the heart of the system, SemiAnalysis said in a post on Monday.\", \"\\\"Kyber NVL144 rack architecture has been delayed to 2028 as the PCB midplane remains challenging from a manufacturability standpoint,\\\" the firm said, referring to a specialized, multi-layer printed circuit board that connects electronic modules within a system.\"]}, {\"ref\": \"gnews:552f8c2bdfd080555d592587#E2\", \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis also said that Nvidia's NVL576, a larger system linking eight racks via optical connections, is likely to be delayed or limited to small volumes.\", \"passages\": [\"NVL576 — a larger system linking eight racks via optical connections — is also likely delayed or limited to small volumes, the research firm said.\"]}, {\"ref\": \"gnews:552f8c2bdfd080555d592587#E3\", \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis reported that Nvidia's backup plan — bolting two of its current-generation racks together to reach similar power — has been scrapped after cloud service providers and hyperscalers pushed back on its awkward design and heavy operational burden.\", \"passages\": [\"A backup plan — bolting two of Nvidia's current-generation racks together for similar power — has also been scrapped after cloud customers rejected the design as awkward and costly to operate.\", \"\\\"It has since been cancelled due to heavy pushback from CSPs [cloud service providers] and hyperscalers over its odd design and heavy operational burden,\\\" SemiAnalysis said.\"]}, {\"ref\": \"gnews:552f8c2bdfd080555d592587#E4\", \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis said that, with the backup design cancelled, Nvidia has \\\"no proven solution to expand the scale-up world size for Rubin Ultra,\\\" and predicted that this could give rivals Advanced Micro Devices and Google — whose in-house chips are already winning business from top AI labs — a rare technical opening at the high end of the market.\", \"passages\": [\"That leaves Nvidia with \\\"no proven solution to expand the scale-up world size for Rubin Ultra,\\\" SemiAnalysis said, predicting that could give rivals Advanced Micro Devices and Google , whose in-house chips are already winning business from top AI labs, a rare technical opening at the high end of the market.\"]}, {\"ref\": \"gnews:552f8c2bdfd080555d592587#E5\", \"summary\": \"Nvidia rejected SemiAnalysis's report of the Kyber delay, stating that \\\"Our roadmap is intact.\\\"\", \"passages\": [\"Nvidia rejected the SemiAnalysis report and said, \\\"Our roadmap is intact.\\\"\"]}, {\"ref\": \"gnews:552f8c2bdfd080555d592587#E6\", \"summary\": \"Nvidia's current-generation Rubin systems are in full production and are to begin shipping this fall (fall 2026) to eight cloud partners, including Amazon Web Services, Microsoft Azure and Google Cloud.\", \"passages\": [\"Nvidia's current-generation Rubin systems are in full production and begin shipping this fall to eight cloud partners, including Amazon Web Services, Microsoft Azure and Google Cloud.\"]}, {\"ref\": \"gnews:552f8c2bdfd080555d592587#E7\", \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis projects that Nvidia's data-center compute revenue will run 20% above Wall Street consensus in the second half of fiscal 2027.\", \"passages\": [\"SemiAnalysis also projects Nvidia's data-center compute revenue will run 20% above Wall Street consensus in the second half of fiscal 2027.\"]}]}\n{\"point\": 11, \"decisive_item\": {\"day\": \"2026-07-06\", \"title\": \"Nvidia's Kyber NVL144 reportedly pushed back more than a year, Asian suppliers drop\", \"who\": \"SemiAnalysis (as reported)\", \"passage\": \"The more powerful version of the upcoming Rubin Ultra chip with four compute dies has also been canceled. Only the smaller two-die version remains, delivering roughly half the real-world compute performance.\", \"meaning\": {\"who\": \"SemiAnalysis\", \"what\": \"four-die Rubin Ultra cancelled; two-die version remains\", \"how_much\": \"roughly half the real-world compute\", \"status\": \"claim\", \"status_note\": \"cancelled\"}}, \"kept_from_that_article\": [{\"ref\": \"gnews:795ea94e1100415a67fd1a96#ev-1\", \"summary\": \"Analyst firm SemiAnalysis reported on X that Nvidia's next-generation AI server rack, Kyber NVL144, has been pushed back more than twelve months to 2028 because of manufacturing problems — specifically the PCB midplane, a central circuit board connecting the rack's components, proving extremely difficult to produce without defects. Jensen Huang had shown off Kyber NVL144 three months earlier at GTC.\", \"passages\": [\"Asian tech stocks slid after analyst firm SemiAnalysis reported on X that Nvidia's next AI server rack system, Kyber NVL144, has been pushed back more than twelve months to 2028 because of manufacturing problems.\", \"The issue, according to SemiAnalysis, is the circuit boards. Specifically, the PCB midplane, a central board that connects all the individual components, has proven extremely difficult to produce without defects. Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang had shown off Kyber NVL144 just three months earlier at the company's GTC conference.\"]}, {\"ref\": \"gnews:795ea94e1100415a67fd1a96#ev-2\", \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis reported that Nvidia scrapped its planned NVL72x2 rack design entirely; the design would have placed two Oberon racks back to back, and cloud providers and large data center operators pushed back against the unusual form factor and high operational overhead.\", \"passages\": [\"A planned alternative design called NVL72x2, which would have placed two Oberon racks back to back, has been scrapped entirely. Cloud providers and large data center operators pushed back against the unusual form factor and the high operational overhead.\"]}, {\"ref\": \"gnews:795ea94e1100415a67fd1a96#ev-3\", \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis reported that Nvidia canceled the more powerful four-compute-die version of its upcoming Rubin Ultra chip; only the smaller two-die version remains, delivering roughly half the real-world compute performance.\", \"passages\": [\"The more powerful version of the upcoming Rubin Ultra chip with four compute dies has also been canceled. Only the smaller two-die version remains, delivering roughly half the real-world compute performance.\"]}, {\"ref\": \"gnews:795ea94e1100415a67fd1a96#ev-4\", \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis reported that Nvidia's CPO-NVSwitch interconnect technology, which links many chips into a single large system, won't arrive until the generation after next (Feynman), meaning Nvidia lacks a proven way to scale Rubin Ultra to very large systems for now; the gap could give competitors such as AMD's MI500X or Google's TPUv8i Broadfly room to move in, and Nvidia plans to make up for the shortfall by selling more Oberon-Rubin racks in the existing form factor.\", \"passages\": [\"A key interconnect technology called CPO-NVSwitch, which links many chips into a single large system, won't arrive until the generation after next, called Feynman. That means Nvidia lacks a proven way to scale Rubin Ultra to very large systems for now. The gap could give competitors like AMD's MI500X or Google's TPUv8i Broadfly room to move in. Nvidia plans to make up for the shortfall by selling more Oberon-Rubin racks in the existing form factor.\"]}]}\n{\"point\": 12, \"decisive_item\": {\"day\": \"2026-07-06\", \"title\": \"Nvidia's Kyber NVL144 reportedly pushed back more than a year, Asian suppliers drop\", \"who\": \"SemiAnalysis (as reported)\", \"passage\": \"Nvidia plans to make up for the shortfall by selling more Oberon-Rubin racks in the existing form factor.\", \"meaning\": {\"who\": \"SemiAnalysis, about NVIDIA's plan\", \"what\": \"sell more Oberon-Rubin racks to cover the Kyber gap\", \"status\": \"plan\", \"status_note\": \"as claimed\"}}, \"kept_from_that_article\": [{\"ref\": \"gnews:795ea94e1100415a67fd1a96#ev-1\", \"summary\": \"Analyst firm SemiAnalysis reported on X that Nvidia's next-generation AI server rack, Kyber NVL144, has been pushed back more than twelve months to 2028 because of manufacturing problems — specifically the PCB midplane, a central circuit board connecting the rack's components, proving extremely difficult to produce without defects. Jensen Huang had shown off Kyber NVL144 three months earlier at GTC.\", \"passages\": [\"Asian tech stocks slid after analyst firm SemiAnalysis reported on X that Nvidia's next AI server rack system, Kyber NVL144, has been pushed back more than twelve months to 2028 because of manufacturing problems.\", \"The issue, according to SemiAnalysis, is the circuit boards. Specifically, the PCB midplane, a central board that connects all the individual components, has proven extremely difficult to produce without defects. Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang had shown off Kyber NVL144 just three months earlier at the company's GTC conference.\"]}, {\"ref\": \"gnews:795ea94e1100415a67fd1a96#ev-2\", \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis reported that Nvidia scrapped its planned NVL72x2 rack design entirely; the design would have placed two Oberon racks back to back, and cloud providers and large data center operators pushed back against the unusual form factor and high operational overhead.\", \"passages\": [\"A planned alternative design called NVL72x2, which would have placed two Oberon racks back to back, has been scrapped entirely. Cloud providers and large data center operators pushed back against the unusual form factor and the high operational overhead.\"]}, {\"ref\": \"gnews:795ea94e1100415a67fd1a96#ev-3\", \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis reported that Nvidia canceled the more powerful four-compute-die version of its upcoming Rubin Ultra chip; only the smaller two-die version remains, delivering roughly half the real-world compute performance.\", \"passages\": [\"The more powerful version of the upcoming Rubin Ultra chip with four compute dies has also been canceled. Only the smaller two-die version remains, delivering roughly half the real-world compute performance.\"]}, {\"ref\": \"gnews:795ea94e1100415a67fd1a96#ev-4\", \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis reported that Nvidia's CPO-NVSwitch interconnect technology, which links many chips into a single large system, won't arrive until the generation after next (Feynman), meaning Nvidia lacks a proven way to scale Rubin Ultra to very large systems for now; the gap could give competitors such as AMD's MI500X or Google's TPUv8i Broadfly room to move in, and Nvidia plans to make up for the shortfall by selling more Oberon-Rubin racks in the existing form factor.\", \"passages\": [\"A key interconnect technology called CPO-NVSwitch, which links many chips into a single large system, won't arrive until the generation after next, called Feynman. That means Nvidia lacks a proven way to scale Rubin Ultra to very large systems for now. The gap could give competitors like AMD's MI500X or Google's TPUv8i Broadfly room to move in. Nvidia plans to make up for the shortfall by selling more Oberon-Rubin racks in the existing form factor.\"]}]}\n{\"point\": 13, \"decisive_item\": {\"day\": \"2026-07-05\", \"title\": \"Nvidia's next-gen AI rack system delayed to 2028 on manufacturing snags, SemiAnalysis says\", \"who\": \"NVIDIA (statement to CNBC)\", \"passage\": \"Our roadmap is intact\", \"meaning\": {\"who\": \"NVIDIA\", \"what\": \"rejects the SemiAnalysis report; says its roadmap (Rubin Ultra / Kyber in 2027) is unchanged\", \"status\": \"claim\"}}, \"kept_from_that_article\": [{\"ref\": \"gnews:552f8c2bdfd080555d592587#E1\", \"summary\": \"Research firm SemiAnalysis reported that Nvidia's Kyber rack-scale architecture (Kyber NVL144), designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips and slated to debut with Vera Rubin Ultra in 2027, has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028, because the multi-layer PCB midplane at the heart of the system remains difficult to manufacture.\", \"passages\": [\"Nvidia 's next marquee product — the Kyber rack-scale architecture designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips — has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028, according to research firm SemiAnalysis, the latest in a string of reported setbacks raising questions about the AI giant's product roadmap.\", \"The design mounts graphics processing units in compute trays that sit vertically instead of horizontally to boost density and reduce latency, and had been slated to debut with Vera Rubin Ultra, Nvidia's next-generation rack-scale system, in 2027 .\", \"The setback stems from difficulties manufacturing a key circuit board at the heart of the system, SemiAnalysis said in a post on Monday.\", \"\\\"Kyber NVL144 rack architecture has been delayed to 2028 as the PCB midplane remains challenging from a manufacturability standpoint,\\\" the firm said, referring to a specialized, multi-layer printed circuit board that connects electronic modules within a system.\"]}, {\"ref\": \"gnews:552f8c2bdfd080555d592587#E2\", \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis also said that Nvidia's NVL576, a larger system linking eight racks via optical connections, is likely to be delayed or limited to small volumes.\", \"passages\": [\"NVL576 — a larger system linking eight racks via optical connections — is also likely delayed or limited to small volumes, the research firm said.\"]}, {\"ref\": \"gnews:552f8c2bdfd080555d592587#E3\", \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis reported that Nvidia's backup plan — bolting two of its current-generation racks together to reach similar power — has been scrapped after cloud service providers and hyperscalers pushed back on its awkward design and heavy operational burden.\", \"passages\": [\"A backup plan — bolting two of Nvidia's current-generation racks together for similar power — has also been scrapped after cloud customers rejected the design as awkward and costly to operate.\", \"\\\"It has since been cancelled due to heavy pushback from CSPs [cloud service providers] and hyperscalers over its odd design and heavy operational burden,\\\" SemiAnalysis said.\"]}, {\"ref\": \"gnews:552f8c2bdfd080555d592587#E4\", \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis said that, with the backup design cancelled, Nvidia has \\\"no proven solution to expand the scale-up world size for Rubin Ultra,\\\" and predicted that this could give rivals Advanced Micro Devices and Google — whose in-house chips are already winning business from top AI labs — a rare technical opening at the high end of the market.\", \"passages\": [\"That leaves Nvidia with \\\"no proven solution to expand the scale-up world size for Rubin Ultra,\\\" SemiAnalysis said, predicting that could give rivals Advanced Micro Devices and Google , whose in-house chips are already winning business from top AI labs, a rare technical opening at the high end of the market.\"]}, {\"ref\": \"gnews:552f8c2bdfd080555d592587#E5\", \"summary\": \"Nvidia rejected SemiAnalysis's report of the Kyber delay, stating that \\\"Our roadmap is intact.\\\"\", \"passages\": [\"Nvidia rejected the SemiAnalysis report and said, \\\"Our roadmap is intact.\\\"\"]}, {\"ref\": \"gnews:552f8c2bdfd080555d592587#E6\", \"summary\": \"Nvidia's current-generation Rubin systems are in full production and are to begin shipping this fall (fall 2026) to eight cloud partners, including Amazon Web Services, Microsoft Azure and Google Cloud.\", \"passages\": [\"Nvidia's current-generation Rubin systems are in full production and begin shipping this fall to eight cloud partners, including Amazon Web Services, Microsoft Azure and Google Cloud.\"]}, {\"ref\": \"gnews:552f8c2bdfd080555d592587#E7\", \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis projects that Nvidia's data-center compute revenue will run 20% above Wall Street consensus in the second half of fiscal 2027.\", \"passages\": [\"SemiAnalysis also projects Nvidia's data-center compute revenue will run 20% above Wall Street consensus in the second half of fiscal 2027.\"]}]}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
